{"id":"AO-NUTRITION-Q81","spec":"ao-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"An 18-month-old boy present to your clinic, his birth weight was 3.5kg & his current weight is 4-5kg (less than the 5thpercentile mother said that he drinks 60ml of humanized infant formula every 4hour, she also feds him a small amount of rice since he was 5 months old, which of the following is sure sign to be present:","o":["Loss of fat from the abdomen","Senile face","Edema of the dorsum of the foot","Fatty hepatomegaly"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"An 18-month-old with very low weight for age/expected growth and bilateral pitting edema would meet a clinical feature of edematous severe acute malnutrition (kwashiorkor phenotype).","more":"Explanation:\nAn 18-month-old with very low weight for age/expected growth and bilateral pitting edema would meet a clinical feature of edematous severe acute malnutrition (kwashiorkor phenotype). Edema may mask the degree of wasting; a full assessment must include MUAC, weight-for-length/height, appetite, infection and complications.\n\nOptions analysis:\na — Loss of abdominal fat is a wasting feature but is not the defining sure sign in an edematous phenotype. | b — 'Senile face' is classically associated with severe wasting/marasmus. | c — Correct: edema of the dorsum of the foot is the hallmark clinical sign of edematous malnutrition. | d — Fatty hepatomegaly may occur in kwashiorkor but is not as direct/consistent as edema.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAn 18-month-old with very low weight for age/expected growth and bilateral pitting edema would meet a clinical feature of edematous severe acute malnutrition (kwashiorkor phenotype).","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q81 — Severe undernutrition with edema","aoSource":"MCQs Lymph Notes.pdf (OCR-derived Markdown), PDF page 24; printed page 23; original Nutrition Q81; answer page(s): same/unclear","aoSystem":"Nutrition","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-NUTRITION-Q82","spec":"ao-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Investigation","q":"A18 month old female infant presenting with recurrent chest infection, she is exclusive breast fed, examination revealed large head, distended abdomen, which of the following investigations will be needed:","o":["High phosphorus","Low calcium& high phosphorus","Low urinary calcium","Low alkaline phosphate"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"In vitamin D deficiency, reduced calcium absorption and secondary hyperparathyroidism conserve calcium in the kidney, so urinary calcium is often low.","more":"Explanation:\nIn vitamin D deficiency, reduced calcium absorption and secondary hyperparathyroidism conserve calcium in the kidney, so urinary calcium is often low. Serum phosphate may be low because of PTH-mediated renal phosphate loss; ALP is usually elevated.\n\nOptions analysis:\na — High phosphate is not the usual nutritional rickets pattern. | b — Low calcium with high phosphorus is not typical. | c — Correct: low urinary calcium can occur as the kidney conserves calcium. | d — ALP is typically elevated, not low.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nIn vitamin D deficiency, reduced calcium absorption and secondary hyperparathyroidism conserve calcium in the kidney, so urinary calcium is often low.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q82 — Urinary calcium in suspected rickets","aoSource":"MCQs Lymph Notes.pdf (OCR-derived Markdown), PDF page 24; printed page 23; original Nutrition Q82; answer page(s): same/unclear","aoSystem":"Nutrition","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-NUTRITION-Q83","spec":"ao-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"A 2year old boy presents with delayed dentition &bone legs. He is exclusive breast fed, he received multiple medications &injections to treat his condition, recently he developed constipation, vomiting& polyuria, what is the most probable diagnosis:","o":["Rickets with hypervitaminosis D","Renal Rickets","Hepatic Rickets","Diabetic Rickets"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"A child with skeletal signs of rickets who develops constipation, vomiting and polyuria after repeated medications/injections may have vitamin D intoxication with hypercalcemia.","more":"Explanation:\nA child with skeletal signs of rickets who develops constipation, vomiting and polyuria after repeated medications/injections may have vitamin D intoxication with hypercalcemia. The diagnosis requires medication/dose history and serum calcium, phosphate, creatinine and 25OHD assessment.\n\nOptions analysis:\na — Correct: rickets complicated by hypervitaminosis D fits the treatment exposure and hypercalcemic symptoms. | b — Renal rickets alone does not explain the symptom onset after repeated vitamin D treatment. | c — Hepatic rickets is not supported. | d — 'Diabetic rickets' is not a standard diagnosis and does not explain the skeletal pattern.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA child with skeletal signs of rickets who develops constipation, vomiting and polyuria after repeated medications/injections may have vitamin D intoxication with hypercalcemia.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q83 — Rickets treatment followed by symptoms of vitamin D toxicity","aoSource":"MCQs Lymph Notes.pdf (OCR-derived Markdown), PDF page 24; printed page 23; original Nutrition Q83; answer page(s): same/unclear","aoSystem":"Nutrition","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-NUTRITION-Q84","spec":"ao-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Investigation","q":"The best investigation to match the previous case is:","o":["Low serum calcium","High serum calcium &ph.","Cardiac calcification","None of above"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Vitamin D toxicity is characterized by hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria and markedly elevated 25OHD; serum phosphate may be elevated, particularly with high vitamin D activity.","more":"Explanation:\nVitamin D toxicity is characterized by hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria and markedly elevated 25OHD; serum phosphate may be elevated, particularly with high vitamin D activity. Cardiac/renal soft-tissue calcification is a complication, not the first biochemical test.\n\nOptions analysis:\na — Low calcium contradicts the expected hypercalcemic toxicity state. | b — Correct source answer: raised calcium and phosphate may occur. | c — Cardiac calcification is a possible complication, not the best investigation. | d — Incorrect because biochemical tests are relevant.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nVitamin D toxicity is characterized by hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria and markedly elevated 25OHD; serum phosphate may be elevated, particularly with high vitamin D activity.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q84 — Investigation in hypervitaminosis D","aoSource":"MCQs Lymph Notes.pdf (OCR-derived Markdown), PDF page 24; printed page 23; original Nutrition Q84; answer page(s): same/unclear","aoSystem":"Nutrition","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-NUTRITION-Q85","spec":"ao-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Complications","q":"A 1-year old infant is complaining of delayed dentition, repeated chest infection, on examination, there is prominent costochondral junction, he is exclusive breast fed, he received multiple investigation for TTT of his condition, all of the following are expected complication for his condition except:","o":["Anorexia","Vomiting","Oliguria","Nephro calcification"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Vitamin D toxicity causes hypercalcemia, gastrointestinal symptoms, polyuria/polydipsia and renal injury including nephrocalcinosis.","more":"Explanation:\nVitamin D toxicity causes hypercalcemia, gastrointestinal symptoms, polyuria/polydipsia and renal injury including nephrocalcinosis. Oliguria is not a typical early manifestation but may occur in advanced renal impairment; it is therefore the intended exception in this exam item.\n\nOptions analysis:\na — Anorexia may occur. | b — Vomiting may occur. | c — Correct intended exception: oliguria is not the usual presenting manifestation; polyuria is more characteristic. | d — Nephrocalcinosis is a recognized complication.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nVitamin D toxicity causes hypercalcemia, gastrointestinal symptoms, polyuria/polydipsia and renal injury including nephrocalcinosis.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q85 — Complications of vitamin D toxicity","aoSource":"MCQs Lymph Notes.pdf (OCR-derived Markdown), PDF page 25; printed page 24; original Nutrition Q85; answer page(s): same/unclear","aoSystem":"Nutrition","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-NUTRITION-Q86-I","spec":"ao-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 20-month-old child complain of acute respiratory infection, the child enters the exam room with a middling genit. Examination reveals prominent forehead, distend abdomen &thickening at the wrist ankle. Plain x-ray film reveals widened epiphyseal plate. Part I: What is the most likely diagnosis (20ll sept):","o":["Vit A deficiency","Hyperparathyroidism","Vit D deficiency","Hypothyroidism"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Prominent forehead, abdominal distension, widened wrists/ankles and widened growth plates are characteristic of rickets.","more":"Explanation:\nProminent forehead, abdominal distension, widened wrists/ankles and widened growth plates are characteristic of rickets. Vitamin D deficiency can produce hypophosphatemia through secondary hyperparathyroidism and renal phosphate wasting. Hypocalcemia may occur, but hypophosphatemia is a common biochemical feature.\n\nOptions analysis:\nPart I: a — Vitamin A deficiency does not cause the radiographic growth-plate widening described. | b — Hyperparathyroidism is a differential but does not best fit the classic infantile rickets phenotype. | c — Correct: vitamin D deficiency. | d — Hypothyroidism may affect growth/dentition but does not explain the rachitic radiograph. Part II: a — Hypocalcemia can occur, but may be normal early. | b — Correct: hypophosphatemia is expected. | c — Hypophosphaturia is opposite to PTH-mediated phosphate wasting. | d — Hyponatremia is not the defining biochemical finding.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nProminent forehead, abdominal distension, widened wrists/ankles and widened growth plates are characteristic of rickets.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q86 — Rickets — clinical and biochemical subparts","aoSource":"MCQs Lymph Notes.pdf (OCR-derived Markdown), PDF page 25; printed page 24; original Nutrition Q86; answer page(s): same/unclear","aoSystem":"Nutrition","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-NUTRITION-Q86-II","spec":"ao-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 20-month-old child complain of acute respiratory infection, the child enters the exam room with a middling genit. Examination reveals prominent forehead, distend abdomen &thickening at the wrist ankle. Plain x-ray film reveals widened epiphyseal plate. Part II: The most likely biochemical findings:","o":["Hypocalcemia","Hypophosphatemia","Hypophosphaturia","Hyponatremia"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Prominent forehead, abdominal distension, widened wrists/ankles and widened growth plates are characteristic of rickets.","more":"Explanation:\nProminent forehead, abdominal distension, widened wrists/ankles and widened growth plates are characteristic of rickets. Vitamin D deficiency can produce hypophosphatemia through secondary hyperparathyroidism and renal phosphate wasting. Hypocalcemia may occur, but hypophosphatemia is a common biochemical feature.\n\nOptions analysis:\nPart I: a — Vitamin A deficiency does not cause the radiographic growth-plate widening described. | b — Hyperparathyroidism is a differential but does not best fit the classic infantile rickets phenotype. | c — Correct: vitamin D deficiency. | d — Hypothyroidism may affect growth/dentition but does not explain the rachitic radiograph. Part II: a — Hypocalcemia can occur, but may be normal early. | b — Correct: hypophosphatemia is expected. | c — Hypophosphaturia is opposite to PTH-mediated phosphate wasting. | d — Hyponatremia is not the defining biochemical finding.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nProminent forehead, abdominal distension, widened wrists/ankles and widened growth plates are characteristic of rickets.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q86 — Rickets — clinical and biochemical subparts","aoSource":"MCQs Lymph Notes.pdf (OCR-derived Markdown), PDF page 25; printed page 24; original Nutrition Q86; answer page(s): same/unclear","aoSystem":"Nutrition","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-NUTRITION-Q87-I","spec":"ao-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"An 8 months old infant come with his parents to clinical, the main complaint is irritability in his left arm, his mother wasn't providing him any supplementation, he been exclusive breast fed since birth. Part I: To manage this infant's irritability (2012 June):","o":["Check his temperature","Do an arm endograph","prepare for CSF examination","Give antispasmodic"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"An exclusively breastfed infant without vitamin D supplementation may be at risk of vitamin D deficiency/rickets and hypocalcemia.","more":"Explanation:\nAn exclusively breastfed infant without vitamin D supplementation may be at risk of vitamin D deficiency/rickets and hypocalcemia. Localized arm irritability could also suggest pain, injury or fracture; evaluation requires examination and appropriate imaging/laboratory testing. The phrase 'arm endograph' is unclear, and the second subquestion's association with iron deficiency is not explained by the stem.\n\nOptions analysis:\nPart I: a — Temperature check is part of general assessment but does not address the localized arm complaint. | b — Source answer B ('arm endograph') is unreadable/uncertain and cannot be confidently interpreted as a specific investigation. | c — CSF examination is not indicated from the provided isolated arm symptom unless CNS infection is suspected. | d — Antispasmodic treatment is not an appropriate diagnostic response. Part II: a — Hydrocephalus is not established by the stem. | b — Generalized edema is not a specific association with this presentation. | c — Source answer C: iron deficiency may coexist in infants with inadequate complementary feeding, but the relationship is not established by this vignette. | d — Atopic edema is not a standard relevant association.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAn exclusively breastfed infant without vitamin D supplementation may be at risk of vitamin D deficiency/rickets and hypocalcemia.\n\nReview note:\nThis item remains in NEEDS REVIEW pending confirmation from the original PDF or the lecturer/source key. The classification is not changed by the educational explanation.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q87 — Infant irritability in an unsupplemented breastfed infant","aoSource":"MCQs Lymph Notes.pdf (OCR-derived Markdown), PDF page 25; printed page 24; original Nutrition Q87; answer page(s): same/unclear","aoSystem":"Nutrition","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-NUTRITION-Q87-II","spec":"ao-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"Possible association include:","o":["Hydrocephalus","Generalized edema","Iron deficiency","Atopic edema"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"An exclusively breastfed infant without vitamin D supplementation may be at risk of vitamin D deficiency/rickets and hypocalcemia.","more":"Explanation:\nAn exclusively breastfed infant without vitamin D supplementation may be at risk of vitamin D deficiency/rickets and hypocalcemia. Localized arm irritability could also suggest pain, injury or fracture; evaluation requires examination and appropriate imaging/laboratory testing. The phrase 'arm endograph' is unclear, and the second subquestion's association with iron deficiency is not explained by the stem.\n\nOptions analysis:\nPart I: a — Temperature check is part of general assessment but does not address the localized arm complaint. | b — Source answer B ('arm endograph') is unreadable/uncertain and cannot be confidently interpreted as a specific investigation. | c — CSF examination is not indicated from the provided isolated arm symptom unless CNS infection is suspected. | d — Antispasmodic treatment is not an appropriate diagnostic response. Part II: a — Hydrocephalus is not established by the stem. | b — Generalized edema is not a specific association with this presentation. | c — Source answer C: iron deficiency may coexist in infants with inadequate complementary feeding, but the relationship is not established by this vignette. | d — Atopic edema is not a standard relevant association.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAn exclusively breastfed infant without vitamin D supplementation may be at risk of vitamin D deficiency/rickets and hypocalcemia.\n\nReview note:\nThis item remains in NEEDS REVIEW pending confirmation from the original PDF or the lecturer/source key. The classification is not changed by the educational explanation.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q87 — Infant irritability in an unsupplemented breastfed infant","aoSource":"MCQs Lymph Notes.pdf (OCR-derived Markdown), PDF page 25; printed page 24; original Nutrition Q87; answer page(s): same/unclear","aoSystem":"Nutrition","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-NUTRITION-Q88","spec":"ao-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 16 months old infant presents to clinical with chest infection &delayed walking, history show exclusive breast feeding with little baby food, on examination distended abdomen &swelling at costochondral junction with leg deformity. What is the cause of delayed walking:","o":["Large head","Abdominal disturbance","Skeletal deformity","hypotension"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Rickets can delay gross motor milestones through bone pain, muscle weakness, deformity of the lower limbs and reduced ability to bear weight.","more":"Explanation:\nRickets can delay gross motor milestones through bone pain, muscle weakness, deformity of the lower limbs and reduced ability to bear weight. When the vignette describes costochondral widening and leg deformity, skeletal disease is the direct explanation for delayed walking.\n\nOptions analysis:\na — A large head may coexist but is not the principal cause of impaired walking. | b — Abdominal distension does not directly explain the motor delay. | c — Correct: skeletal deformity and painful/weak bones impede standing and walking. | d — Hypotension is not suggested by the findings.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nRickets can delay gross motor milestones through bone pain, muscle weakness, deformity of the lower limbs and reduced ability to bear weight.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q88 — Skeletal deformity causing delayed walking","aoSource":"MCQs Lymph Notes.pdf (OCR-derived Markdown), PDF page 26; printed page 25; original Nutrition Q88; answer page(s): same/unclear","aoSystem":"Nutrition","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-NUTRITION-Q9","spec":"ao-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"compared to human milk, cow's milk has higher: 2017","o":["proteins","calories","water","whey to casein ratio"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Unmodified cow's milk contains substantially more total protein and a different whey-to-casein profile than human milk.","more":"Explanation:\nUnmodified cow's milk contains substantially more total protein and a different whey-to-casein profile than human milk. Human milk contains more lactose and is adapted to the infant's renal and digestive capacity.\n\nOptions analysis:\na — Correct: cow's milk has higher protein concentration. | b — The energy density is not reliably higher than human milk; both are broadly similar in energy per volume. | c — Cow's milk has less water proportionally, not more. | d — Human milk has a higher whey-to-casein ratio, especially early in lactation.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nUnmodified cow's milk contains substantially more total protein and a different whey-to-casein profile than human milk.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q9 — Cow's milk compared with human milk","aoSource":"MCQs Lymph Notes.pdf (OCR-derived Markdown), PDF page 13; printed page 12; original Nutrition Q9; answer page(s): same/unclear","aoSystem":"Nutrition","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q1","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"All the following anti-tuberculous drugs are given orally, except:","o":["Isoniazid.","Rifampicin.","Pyrazinamide.","Streptomycin."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Isoniazid, rifampicin and pyrazinamide are oral drugs.","more":"Explanation:\nIsoniazid, rifampicin and pyrazinamide are oral drugs. Streptomycin is an aminoglycoside historically administered by intramuscular injection, not orally. Streptomycin is not part of routine contemporary first-line regimens for drug-susceptible childhood TB in many current protocols; use a pediatric TB regimen selected by an experienced TB team and drug-susceptibility data.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Isoniazid.** — The other three drugs are orally absorbed first-line agents; streptomycin is injectable and is now used selectively, not as a routine first-line drug.\n- **b. Rifampicin.** — The other three drugs are orally absorbed first-line agents; streptomycin is injectable and is now used selectively, not as a routine first-line drug.\n- **c. Pyrazinamide.** — The other three drugs are orally absorbed first-line agents; streptomycin is injectable and is now used selectively, not as a routine first-line drug.\n- **d. Streptomycin.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Streptomycin is administered parenterally (classically IM), unlike the listed oral agents.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nDo not infer a modern TB regimen from an old route-of-administration MCQ.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q1 — Route of administration of first-line and historical anti-TB drugs","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 97 · answer PDF p. 97 · original question Q1","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q10","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Investigation","q":"False negative tuberculin test may happen in the following situation except:","o":["Cachexia e.g. marasmus.","Cell mediated immunodeficiency. . Recent use of corticosteroids.","Obesity."],"ol":["A","B","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Anergy or reduced cell-mediated immunity can produce a false-negative tuberculin skin test despite TB infection.","more":"Explanation:\nAnergy or reduced cell-mediated immunity can produce a false-negative tuberculin skin test despite TB infection. Severe malnutrition/cachexia, immunodeficiency and corticosteroid exposure can blunt delayed-type hypersensitivity. Obesity is not a standard cause of a false-negative TST, making it the exception in this item.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Cachexia e.g. marasmus.** — Cachexia, impaired cell-mediated immunity and corticosteroids can reduce the delayed hypersensitivity response and yield false-negative tests.\n- **b. Cell mediated immunodeficiency. . Recent use of corticosteroids.** — Cachexia, impaired cell-mediated immunity and corticosteroids can reduce the delayed hypersensitivity response and yield false-negative tests.\n- **d. Obesity.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Obesity is not a usual cause of a false-negative TST.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAnergy or reduced cell-mediated immunity can produce a false-negative tuberculin skin test despite TB infection.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q10 — False-negative tuberculin skin test: obesity is not a usual cause","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 99 · answer PDF p. 99 · original question Q10","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q11","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"Ghon's focus is:","o":["Primary complex.","Miliary TB.","TB cavitation.","TB lymphadenitis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The Ghon focus is the initial parenchymal lesion of primary pulmonary TB, usually subpleural.","more":"Explanation:\nThe Ghon focus is the initial parenchymal lesion of primary pulmonary TB, usually subpleural. The primary (Ghon) complex more broadly comprises the Ghon focus plus lymphatic spread and regional hilar lymph-node involvement. The MCQ treats “Ghon focus” and “primary complex” as synonyms; that is common in simplified teaching but anatomically imprecise.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Primary complex. b Miliary TB.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. A Ghon focus is the primary parenchymal lesion; a primary complex includes the focus and regional lymph-node/lymphatic involvement.\n- **c. TB cavitation.** — Miliary TB, cavitation and isolated TB lymphadenitis are distinct patterns and do not define the Ghon focus.\n- **d. TB lymphadenitis.** — Miliary TB, cavitation and isolated TB lymphadenitis are distinct patterns and do not define the Ghon focus.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe Ghon focus is the initial parenchymal lesion of primary pulmonary TB, usually subpleural.\n\nReview note:\nRetain the source key, but distinguish Ghon focus from Ghon/primary complex in teaching.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q11 — Ghon focus versus primary complex: terminology is imprecise","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 99 · answer PDF p. 99 · original question Q11","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q12","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"Which of the following is correct about otitis media:","o":["Hemophilus influenza is one of the common causative agents.","Rarely seen below 1st year of life.","Young children are more prone due to their long Eustachian tubes.","Oral penicillin V should be given for all patients."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Acute otitis media commonly follows viral upper respiratory infection and is caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae, non-typeable Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis.","more":"Explanation:\nAcute otitis media commonly follows viral upper respiratory infection and is caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae, non-typeable Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis. H. influenzae is therefore a valid common cause. AOM is particularly common in infancy and early childhood because the Eustachian tube is shorter, more horizontal and functionally less efficient—not longer. Treatment is selected according to age, severity, laterality, otorrhea and follow-up reliability.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Hemophilus influenza is one of the common causative agents.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Haemophilus influenzae is one of the common AOM pathogens.\n- **b. Rarely seen below 1st year of life.** — AOM is common in infants; the Eustachian tube is shorter and more horizontal in young children; penicillin V is not universal first-line treatment and does not cover beta-lactamase-producing H. influenzae.\n- **c. Young children are more prone due to their long Eustachian tubes.** — AOM is common in infants; the Eustachian tube is shorter and more horizontal in young children; penicillin V is not universal first-line treatment and does not cover beta-lactamase-producing H. influenzae.\n- **d. Oral penicillin V should be given for all patients.** — AOM is common in infants; the Eustachian tube is shorter and more horizontal in young children; penicillin V is not universal first-line treatment and does not cover beta-lactamase-producing H. influenzae.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAcute otitis media commonly follows viral upper respiratory infection and is caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae, non-typeable Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q12 — Otitis media: common bacterial pathogens","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 99 · answer PDF p. 99 · original question Q12","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q13","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Features","q":"All the following about bronchiectasis in children is true except:","o":["There is destruction of ciliated epithelium with loss of elastic tissue.","Can be congenital or acquired.","Clubbing of fingers is rare.","Anorexia, fever and poor weight gain are common."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Bronchiectasis is permanent abnormal dilatation of bronchi due to chronic infection/inflammation and destruction of elastic and muscular airway components.","more":"Explanation:\nBronchiectasis is permanent abnormal dilatation of bronchi due to chronic infection/inflammation and destruction of elastic and muscular airway components. It may be congenital or acquired. Chronic productive cough, recurrent infections, anorexia, fever, poor growth and digital clubbing can occur. Clubbing is a recognized finding, so “clubbing is rare” is the false statement in the source key.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. There is destruction of ciliated epithelium with loss of elastic tissue.** — Airway destruction and both congenital/acquired causes are consistent with bronchiectasis; poor growth and chronic infective symptoms are common.\n- **b. Can be congenital or acquired.** — Airway destruction and both congenital/acquired causes are consistent with bronchiectasis; poor growth and chronic infective symptoms are common.\n- **c. Clubbing of fingers is rare.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Digital clubbing is a recognized feature of established pediatric bronchiectasis; it is not rare.\n- **d. Anorexia, fever and poor weight gain are common.** — Airway destruction and both congenital/acquired causes are consistent with bronchiectasis; poor growth and chronic infective symptoms are common.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nBronchiectasis is permanent abnormal dilatation of bronchi due to chronic infection/inflammation and destruction of elastic and muscular airway components.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q13 — Bronchiectasis: digital clubbing is not rare","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 99 · answer PDF p. 99 · original question Q13","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q14-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"A 6-year-old boy was brought to his physician because of progressively increasing respiratory distress over the last day. His temperature is 37.6°C, respiratory rate is 65/minute with a bluish tinge of his lips. Auscultation revealed minimal wheezing and diminished air entry bilaterally. Part I: The patient should be:","o":["Treated at home with oral salbutamol.","Treated at home with nebulized salbutamol.","Treated at home with inhaled corticosteroids.","Immediately hospitalized."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Progressive distress, marked tachypnea, cyanosis and diminished bilateral air entry are danger signs.","more":"Explanation:\nProgressive distress, marked tachypnea, cyanosis and diminished bilateral air entry are danger signs. A child with these findings requires immediate emergency treatment and hospital assessment, not home bronchodilator-only management. In a severe acute asthma attack, administer oxygen when hypoxemic, repeated inhaled/nebulized short-acting beta-2 agonist, add ipratropium when indicated, and give early systemic corticosteroid. Among the listed long-term prophylactic agents, sodium cromoglycate, inhaled budesonide and montelukast are controller/preventer drugs; oral salbutamol is not an appropriate prophylactic controller.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nProgressive distress, marked tachypnea, cyanosis and diminished bilateral air entry are danger signs.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q14 — Severe asthma or acute respiratory compromise requires hospital care","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 100 · answer PDF p. 100 · original question Q14","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q14-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"A 6-year-old boy was brought to his physician because of progressively increasing respiratory distress over the last day. His temperature is 37.6°C, respiratory rate is 65/minute with a bluish tinge of his lips. Auscultation revealed minimal wheezing and diminished air entry bilaterally. Part II: The following is not a prophylactic agent:","o":["Inhaled sodium cromoglycate.","Inhaled budesonide.","Montelukast.","Oral salbutamol."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Progressive distress, marked tachypnea, cyanosis and diminished bilateral air entry are danger signs.","more":"Explanation:\nProgressive distress, marked tachypnea, cyanosis and diminished bilateral air entry are danger signs. A child with these findings requires immediate emergency treatment and hospital assessment, not home bronchodilator-only management. In a severe acute asthma attack, administer oxygen when hypoxemic, repeated inhaled/nebulized short-acting beta-2 agonist, add ipratropium when indicated, and give early systemic corticosteroid. Among the listed long-term prophylactic agents, sodium cromoglycate, inhaled budesonide and montelukast are controller/preventer drugs; oral salbutamol is not an appropriate prophylactic controller.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nProgressive distress, marked tachypnea, cyanosis and diminished bilateral air entry are danger signs.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q14 — Severe asthma or acute respiratory compromise requires hospital care","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 100 · answer PDF p. 100 · original question Q14","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q15","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Investigation","q":"All of the following statements about tuberculosis in children are true, except:","o":["Chest TB is more common than other types.","Tuberculin test is always positive in any tuberculous child.","Should be included in all differential diagnosis of chronic chest problems.","BCG doesn't give full protection against TB."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"A positive TST is not universal in pediatric TB.","more":"Explanation:\nA positive TST is not universal in pediatric TB. False-negative results occur with severe disease, young age, immunosuppression, malnutrition, recent infection before conversion, and technical factors. Pulmonary TB is common in children, and BCG offers variable, incomplete protection, particularly against pulmonary disease; it is more effective at preventing severe disseminated childhood TB.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Chest TB is more common than other types.** — Chest TB is a common form; TB belongs in the differential for chronic respiratory illness; BCG does not provide complete protection.\n- **b. Tuberculin test is always positive in any tuberculous child.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. The TST is not always positive in a child with active TB.\n- **c. Should be included in all differential diagnosis of chronic chest problems.** — Chest TB is a common form; TB belongs in the differential for chronic respiratory illness; BCG does not provide complete protection.\n- **d. BCG doesn't give full protection against TB.** — Chest TB is a common form; TB belongs in the differential for chronic respiratory illness; BCG does not provide complete protection.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA positive TST is not universal in pediatric TB.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q15 — Tuberculin test may be negative in a child with TB","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 100 · answer PDF p. 100 · original question Q15","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q16","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"All the following are true about acute bronchiolitis, except:","o":["Most cases are under 2 years old.","Most cases are caused by RSV.","Antibiotics are recommended in infants below 3 months age.","Steroids maybe helpful in some cases."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Bronchiolitis is an acute viral lower respiratory tract infection, usually in children younger than 2 years and most commonly associated with RSV.","more":"Explanation:\nBronchiolitis is an acute viral lower respiratory tract infection, usually in children younger than 2 years and most commonly associated with RSV. Management is mainly supportive: hydration, nasal clearance when needed, and oxygen for clinically significant hypoxemia. Routine antibiotics are not indicated unless bacterial infection is suspected or documented. Systemic corticosteroids are not recommended for routine bronchiolitis. Therefore both option c (routine antibiotics solely because age is below 3 months) and option d (“steroids may be helpful”) are not reliable statements as worded, making the one-best-answer format problematic.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Most cases are under 2 years old.** — Age and RSV association are correct; routine antibiotics in young infants without bacterial infection are not recommended; systemic steroids are not recommended in bronchiolitis.\n- **b. Most cases are caused by RSV.** — Age and RSV association are correct; routine antibiotics in young infants without bacterial infection are not recommended; systemic steroids are not recommended in bronchiolitis.\n- **c. Antibiotics are recommended in infants below 3 months age.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. RSV is the commonest cause; routine antibiotics and systemic corticosteroids are not recommended for uncomplicated bronchiolitis.\n- **d. Steroids maybe helpful in some cases.** — Age and RSV association are correct; routine antibiotics in young infants without bacterial infection are not recommended; systemic steroids are not recommended in bronchiolitis.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nBronchiolitis is an acute viral lower respiratory tract infection, usually in children younger than 2 years and most commonly associated with RSV.\n\nReview note:\nNeeds Review because the source’s “except” item has at least two incorrect choices under current guideline-based practice.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q16 — Bronchiolitis: source item contains more than one false management statement","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 100 · answer PDF p. 100 · original question Q16","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q17","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"One of the following is not a first line of anti-tuberculous drugs:","o":["Kanamycin.","Isoniazid.","Pyrazinamide.","Rifampicin."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The standard first-line drugs for drug-susceptible TB are isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol (HRZE), with regimen length and composition modified by disease severity, age and drug susceptibility.","more":"Explanation:\nThe standard first-line drugs for drug-susceptible TB are isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol (HRZE), with regimen length and composition modified by disease severity, age and drug susceptibility. Kanamycin is an injectable aminoglycoside historically used in drug-resistant TB; it is not a routine first-line drug and has significant toxicity concerns.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Kanamycin.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Kanamycin is not a standard first-line anti-TB drug.\n- **b. Isoniazid.** — Isoniazid, pyrazinamide and rifampicin are core first-line drugs in drug-susceptible TB regimens.\n- **c. Pyrazinamide.** — Isoniazid, pyrazinamide and rifampicin are core first-line drugs in drug-susceptible TB regimens.\n- **d. Rifampicin.** — Isoniazid, pyrazinamide and rifampicin are core first-line drugs in drug-susceptible TB regimens.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe standard first-line drugs for drug-susceptible TB are isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol (HRZE), with regimen length and composition modified by disease severity, age and drug susceptibility.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q17 — Kanamycin is not a first-line anti-tuberculous drug","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 100 · answer PDF p. 100 · original question Q17","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q18","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Features","q":"One of the following is true about bronchiolitis:","o":["It is a common cause of wheezing in infants.","Mainly presents by harsh expiratory sounds.","Corticosteroids therapy is often useful.","Family history of atopy is usually present."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Bronchiolitis is common in infants and typically follows coryza with cough, tachypnea, crackles and/or wheeze.","more":"Explanation:\nBronchiolitis is common in infants and typically follows coryza with cough, tachypnea, crackles and/or wheeze. Wheezing is common but not mandatory. It is not an atopic disease, corticosteroids are not routinely useful, and a family history of atopy is not required for diagnosis.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. It is a common cause of wheezing in infants.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Bronchiolitis is a common cause of infant wheeze.\n- **b. Mainly presents by harsh expiratory sounds.** — Harsh expiratory sounds are not its defining feature; corticosteroids are not routine treatment; atopy is not required.\n- **c. Corticosteroids therapy is often useful.** — Harsh expiratory sounds are not its defining feature; corticosteroids are not routine treatment; atopy is not required.\n- **d. Family history of atopy is usually present.** — Harsh expiratory sounds are not its defining feature; corticosteroids are not routine treatment; atopy is not required.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nBronchiolitis is common in infants and typically follows coryza with cough, tachypnea, crackles and/or wheeze.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q18 — Bronchiolitis is a common cause of wheezing in infants","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 100 · answer PDF p. 100 · original question Q18","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q19","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"In tuberculosis:","o":["Bovine bacilli never cause infection to human.","Ghon's focus is seen in primary complex.","BCG is prepared from killed mycobacterium tuberculosis.","Pulmonary affection in primary complex is very rare."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"The primary TB lesion is a subpleural parenchymal focus (Ghon focus), commonly with lymphatic spread to regional hilar nodes.","more":"Explanation:\nThe primary TB lesion is a subpleural parenchymal focus (Ghon focus), commonly with lymphatic spread to regional hilar nodes. Bovine M. bovis can infect humans, especially via unpasteurized dairy products. BCG is a live attenuated M. bovis vaccine strain, not killed M. tuberculosis. Pulmonary disease is not rare in primary TB.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Bovine bacilli never cause infection to human.** — M. bovis can infect humans; BCG is live attenuated; pulmonary involvement is common in primary TB.\n- **b. Ghon's focus is seen in primary complex.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Ghon focus is the primary parenchymal lesion associated with the primary complex.\n- **c. BCG is prepared from killed mycobacterium tuberculosis.** — M. bovis can infect humans; BCG is live attenuated; pulmonary involvement is common in primary TB.\n- **d. Pulmonary affection in primary complex is very rare.** — M. bovis can infect humans; BCG is live attenuated; pulmonary involvement is common in primary TB.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe primary TB lesion is a subpleural parenchymal focus (Ghon focus), commonly with lymphatic spread to regional hilar nodes.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q19 — Ghon focus is part of the primary TB complex","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 100 · answer PDF p. 101 · original question Q19","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q2","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Investigation","q":"Regarding tuberculin test, which of the following statement is NOT correct:","o":["The dose of purified protein derivative (PPD) injected is 0.1 ml.","PPD is injected intra-dermally.","The reaction should be read within 36 hours.","It is the induration and not the erythema that counts in the interpretation."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"The Mantoux test uses 0.1 mL of purified protein derivative injected intradermally.","more":"Explanation:\nThe Mantoux test uses 0.1 mL of purified protein derivative injected intradermally. It is read by measuring transverse induration—not erythema—at 48–72 hours. The source answer c is consistent with current CDC guidance because reading at 36 hours is too early.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. The dose of purified protein derivative (PPD) injected is 0.1 ml.** — 0.1 mL is the usual PPD volume; the intradermal route is correct; timing is 48–72 hours; erythema is not measured.\n- **b. PPD is injected intra-dermally.** — 0.1 mL is the usual PPD volume; the intradermal route is correct; timing is 48–72 hours; erythema is not measured.\n- **c. The reaction should be read within 36 hours.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Read the induration at 48–72 hours; a 36-hour reading is not the standard reading window.\n- **d. It is the induration and not the erythema that counts in the interpretation.** — 0.1 mL is the usual PPD volume; the intradermal route is correct; timing is 48–72 hours; erythema is not measured.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nInterpretation threshold depends on risk category and immune status, not simply on the diameter alone.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q2 — Tuberculin skin test: correct timing and interpretation","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 97 · answer PDF p. 97 · original question Q2","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q20-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 5-year-old boy presents with a 1-week-history of fever, anorexia, productive cough, and right upper quadrant abdominal pain. On examination, he is febrile, short of breath, with mild subcostal and intercostal recessions. Auscultation reveals decreased breath sounds and bronchial breathing on the right side of her chest. What is the most likely diagnosis:","o":["Viral hepatitis.","Right lower lobe pneumonia.","Acute appendicitis.","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"A child with fever, productive cough, dyspnea, focal bronchial breathing and reduced air entry has pneumonia.","more":"Explanation:\nA child with fever, productive cough, dyspnea, focal bronchial breathing and reduced air entry has pneumonia. Basal or lower-lobe pneumonia can irritate the diaphragm and produce referred abdominal pain, including right upper quadrant pain. Treatment is guided by severity, oxygenation, age, likely pathogen and imaging when indicated; surgery is not routine management of uncomplicated pneumonia.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA child with fever, productive cough, dyspnea, focal bronchial breathing and reduced air entry has pneumonia.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q20 — Pneumonia can present with referred right upper quadrant pain","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 101 · answer PDF p. 101 · original question Q20","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q20-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 5-year-old boy presents with a 1-week-history of fever, anorexia, productive cough, and right upper quadrant abdominal pain. On examination, he is febrile, short of breath, with mild subcostal and intercostal recessions. Auscultation reveals decreased breath sounds and bronchial breathing on the right side of her chest. What is the most likely diagnosis:","o":["Viral hepatitis.","Right lower lobe pneumonia.","Acute appendicitis.","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"A child with fever, productive cough, dyspnea, focal bronchial breathing and reduced air entry has pneumonia.","more":"Explanation:\nA child with fever, productive cough, dyspnea, focal bronchial breathing and reduced air entry has pneumonia. Basal or lower-lobe pneumonia can irritate the diaphragm and produce referred abdominal pain, including right upper quadrant pain. Treatment is guided by severity, oxygenation, age, likely pathogen and imaging when indicated; surgery is not routine management of uncomplicated pneumonia.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA child with fever, productive cough, dyspnea, focal bronchial breathing and reduced air entry has pneumonia.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q20 — Pneumonia can present with referred right upper quadrant pain","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 101 · answer PDF p. 101 · original question Q20","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q21","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Regarding cystic fibrosis, which is wrong:","o":["Autosomal recessive disease.","Failure to thrive and malabsorption are common features.","Recurrent chest infections are common.","Respiratory secretions are lower in viscosity."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"CFTR dysfunction reduces chloride/bicarbonate transport and alters airway-surface liquid, producing dehydrated, viscous mucus and impaired mucociliary clearance.","more":"Explanation:\nCFTR dysfunction reduces chloride/bicarbonate transport and alters airway-surface liquid, producing dehydrated, viscous mucus and impaired mucociliary clearance. This promotes chronic infection, inflammation and bronchiectasis. CF is autosomal recessive and often causes pancreatic exocrine insufficiency and failure to thrive.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Autosomal recessive disease.** — Autosomal recessive inheritance, malabsorption/failure to thrive and recurrent chest infection are typical CF features.\n- **b. Failure to thrive and malabsorption are common features.** — Autosomal recessive inheritance, malabsorption/failure to thrive and recurrent chest infection are typical CF features.\n- **c. Recurrent chest infections are common.** — Autosomal recessive inheritance, malabsorption/failure to thrive and recurrent chest infection are typical CF features.\n- **d. Respiratory secretions are lower in viscosity.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Respiratory secretions in CF are abnormally viscous, not less viscous.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nCFTR dysfunction reduces chloride/bicarbonate transport and alters airway-surface liquid, producing dehydrated, viscous mucus and impaired mucociliary clearance.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q21 — Cystic fibrosis causes increased viscosity of respiratory secretions","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 101 · answer PDF p. 101 · original question Q21","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q22-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 4-year-old girl was brought to the ER with temp 39.8°C and breathing difficulty. On examination, the girl looked toxic though of normal consciousness, RR 64/min, working ala nasi, intercostal retractions and grunting. Her O2 saturation at room air was 90%. Chest x-ray showed opacity in right upper lobe with central trachea. Part I: The grade of respiratory distress is:","o":["Grade l.","Grade 2.","Grade 3.","Grade 4."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Tachypnea, nasal flaring, intercostal retractions, grunting and oxygen saturation of 90% indicate significant respiratory distress.","more":"Explanation:\nTachypnea, nasal flaring, intercostal retractions, grunting and oxygen saturation of 90% indicate significant respiratory distress. A focal right upper-lobe opacity with a central trachea is consistent with lobar consolidation rather than major volume-loss collapse. The answer to the diagnosis portion is lobar pneumonia. The numeric grade cannot be independently validated because the stem does not identify the grading scale.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nTachypnea, nasal flaring, intercostal retractions, grunting and oxygen saturation of 90% indicate significant respiratory distress.\n\nReview note:\nThe parent item remains Needs Review because one part uses an undefined distress scale. The pneumonia diagnosis can still be taught as above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q22 — Respiratory distress grade and lobar pneumonia: grade scale not named","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 101 · answer PDF p. 101 · original question Q22","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q22-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 4-year-old girl was brought to the ER with temp 39.8°C and breathing difficulty. On examination, the girl looked toxic though of normal consciousness, RR 64/min, working ala nasi, intercostal retractions and grunting. Her O2 saturation at room air was 90%. Chest x-ray showed opacity in right upper lobe with central trachea. Part II: The most likely diagnosis is:","o":["Bronchopneumonia.","Lobar pneumonia.","Right upper lobar collapse.","Foreign body aspiration."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Tachypnea, nasal flaring, intercostal retractions, grunting and oxygen saturation of 90% indicate significant respiratory distress.","more":"Explanation:\nTachypnea, nasal flaring, intercostal retractions, grunting and oxygen saturation of 90% indicate significant respiratory distress. A focal right upper-lobe opacity with a central trachea is consistent with lobar consolidation rather than major volume-loss collapse. The answer to the diagnosis portion is lobar pneumonia. The numeric grade cannot be independently validated because the stem does not identify the grading scale.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nTachypnea, nasal flaring, intercostal retractions, grunting and oxygen saturation of 90% indicate significant respiratory distress.\n\nReview note:\nThe parent item remains Needs Review because one part uses an undefined distress scale. The pneumonia diagnosis can still be taught as above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q22 — Respiratory distress grade and lobar pneumonia: grade scale not named","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 101 · answer PDF p. 101 · original question Q22","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q23","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"Which of the following is correct about lung abscess:","o":["Bronchoscopy is used to aspirate pus.","Antibiotic therapy for 6 weeks is usually indicated.","Metastatic lung abscess is common in children.","Its clinical picture starts acutely."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"A lung abscess is a localized necrotizing infection with a pus-filled cavity.","more":"Explanation:\nA lung abscess is a localized necrotizing infection with a pus-filled cavity. Pediatric management generally requires prolonged pathogen-directed antibiotics, often several weeks, with duration guided by clinical and radiological response. Bronchoscopy is not routinely performed simply to aspirate pus; it may be used if foreign body obstruction, atypical infection or failure to improve is suspected. Metastatic abscess is not the common pediatric pattern.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Bronchoscopy is used to aspirate pus.** — Bronchoscopy is not routine pus aspiration; metastatic abscess is uncommon; presentation can be subacute rather than invariably acute.\n- **b. Antibiotic therapy for 6 weeks is usually indicated.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. A prolonged course of antibiotics (often around 4–6 weeks, individualized) is generally required.\n- **c. Metastatic lung abscess is common in children.** — Bronchoscopy is not routine pus aspiration; metastatic abscess is uncommon; presentation can be subacute rather than invariably acute.\n- **d. Its clinical picture starts acutely.** — Bronchoscopy is not routine pus aspiration; metastatic abscess is uncommon; presentation can be subacute rather than invariably acute.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA lung abscess is a localized necrotizing infection with a pus-filled cavity.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q23 — Lung abscess usually requires prolonged antibiotic therapy","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 101 · answer PDF p. 102 · original question Q23","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q24","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"The commonest cause of pneumonia in infancy is:","o":["Tuberculosis.","Aspiration.","Viral.","Pneumococcal."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Viruses are major causes of lower respiratory infection in infants, particularly in community settings.","more":"Explanation:\nViruses are major causes of lower respiratory infection in infants, particularly in community settings. The relative proportion of viral and bacterial pneumonia depends on age, vaccination, season, setting and diagnostic method. Pneumococcus is an important bacterial cause, but it is not the overall most frequent cause in every infant population.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Tuberculosis.** — TB is not the commonest general cause; aspiration is a mechanism in selected infants; pneumococcus is a leading bacterial pathogen but not all-cause etiology.\n- **b. Aspiration.** — TB is not the commonest general cause; aspiration is a mechanism in selected infants; pneumococcus is a leading bacterial pathogen but not all-cause etiology.\n- **c. Viral.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Viral infection is a major and often predominant cause of infant pneumonia.\n- **d. Pneumococcal.** — TB is not the commonest general cause; aspiration is a mechanism in selected infants; pneumococcus is a leading bacterial pathogen but not all-cause etiology.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nViruses are major causes of lower respiratory infection in infants, particularly in community settings.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q24 — Viral causes predominate among pneumonia in infancy","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 102 · answer PDF p. 102 · original question Q24","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q25","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"Suppurative lung diseases include:","o":["Asthma.","Bronchiolitis.","Emphysema.","Empyema."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Suppurative pulmonary diseases include lung abscess, bronchiectasis and empyema.","more":"Explanation:\nSuppurative pulmonary diseases include lung abscess, bronchiectasis and empyema. Empyema is infected/purulent fluid in the pleural space, usually arising as a complication of pneumonia. Asthma and bronchiolitis are obstructive/inflammatory airway disorders, while emphysema is an alveolar destruction process.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Asthma.** — Asthma and bronchiolitis are airway disorders; emphysema is not classed as a suppurative lung disease.\n- **b. Bronchiolitis.** — Asthma and bronchiolitis are airway disorders; emphysema is not classed as a suppurative lung disease.\n- **c. Emphysema.** — Asthma and bronchiolitis are airway disorders; emphysema is not classed as a suppurative lung disease.\n- **d. Empyema.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Empyema is a suppurative pleural disease.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nSuppurative pulmonary diseases include lung abscess, bronchiectasis and empyema.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q25 — Empyema is a suppurative lung/pleural infection","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 102 · answer PDF p. 102 · original question Q25","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q26-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Treatment","q":"A 4-year-old girl suffered from fever and mild tachypnea for 2 days. She was treated with oral amoxicillin in a dose of 50 mg/kg/day in 3 divided doses. Two days later, her respiratory distress and fever were getting worse. Chest x-ray revealed a lung abscess occupying the left with patchy lung consolidation. Part I: The most probable organism is:","o":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis.","Staphylococcus aureus.","Respiratory syncytial virus.","Adenovirus."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"A child worsening with a radiological lung abscess despite initial beta-lactam therapy raises concern for Staphylococcus aureus, including MRSA depending on local epidemiology.","more":"Explanation:\nA child worsening with a radiological lung abscess despite initial beta-lactam therapy raises concern for Staphylococcus aureus, including MRSA depending on local epidemiology. Vancomycin is an option when serious MRSA infection is suspected, but definitive therapy should be culture-guided and local resistance patterns matter. The source dose of amoxicillin is not sufficient to determine that all treatment failure is due to resistance; imaging, cultures and specialist review are appropriate.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA lung abscess requires tailored prolonged antimicrobial therapy; reassess for obstruction/foreign body and complications.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q26 — Staphylococcal lung abscess: consider anti-staphylococcal coverage","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 102 · answer PDF p. 102 · original question Q26","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q26-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Treatment","q":"A 4-year-old girl suffered from fever and mild tachypnea for 2 days. She was treated with oral amoxicillin in a dose of 50 mg/kg/day in 3 divided doses. Two days later, her respiratory distress and fever were getting worse. Chest x-ray revealed a lung abscess occupying the left with patchy lung consolidation. Part II: The following antibiotic should be added:","o":["Tetracycline.","Ceftriaxone.","Vancomycin.","Rifampicin + Isoniazid + Pyrazinamide."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"A child worsening with a radiological lung abscess despite initial beta-lactam therapy raises concern for Staphylococcus aureus, including MRSA depending on local epidemiology.","more":"Explanation:\nA child worsening with a radiological lung abscess despite initial beta-lactam therapy raises concern for Staphylococcus aureus, including MRSA depending on local epidemiology. Vancomycin is an option when serious MRSA infection is suspected, but definitive therapy should be culture-guided and local resistance patterns matter. The source dose of amoxicillin is not sufficient to determine that all treatment failure is due to resistance; imaging, cultures and specialist review are appropriate.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA lung abscess requires tailored prolonged antimicrobial therapy; reassess for obstruction/foreign body and complications.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q26 — Staphylococcal lung abscess: consider anti-staphylococcal coverage","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 102 · answer PDF p. 102 · original question Q26","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q27-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 4-year-old girl presented to the ER with severe grade 3 respiratory distress with slight bluish tinge in the lips on crying. The girl looked toxic, her temp. was 39.6°C, HR 128 bpm and RR 42/min. Her mother gave a history of high fever in the last 4 days and severe cough. Chest examination revealed diminished air entry over the right lung, scattered fine consonating crepitations, and patch of bronchial breathing. The left lung was to totally normal. Chest x-ray revealed a total opacification of her right lung with no medication shift. CBC showed leukocytosis, shift to left and toxic granulations. Part I: The most likely diagnosis:","o":["Acute bronchitis","Acute bronchiolitis","Bacterial pneumonia","Pneumonia complicated with pleural effusion."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"High fever, toxic appearance, focal bronchial breathing, leukocytosis with left shift and lobar consolidation support bacterial pneumonia.","more":"Explanation:\nHigh fever, toxic appearance, focal bronchial breathing, leukocytosis with left shift and lobar consolidation support bacterial pneumonia. Hypoxemia and raised CRP may be present. Oxygen and appropriate antibiotics are indicated according to severity. Mechanical ventilation is not automatic solely because there is severe pneumonia; it is reserved for respiratory failure or inability to maintain ventilation/oxygenation despite support.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nHigh fever, toxic appearance, focal bronchial breathing, leukocytosis with left shift and lobar consolidation support bacterial pneumonia.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q27 — Bacterial pneumonia with lobar opacification","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 103 · answer PDF p. 103 · original question Q27","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q27-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 4-year-old girl presented to the ER with severe grade 3 respiratory distress with slight bluish tinge in the lips on crying. The girl looked toxic, her temp. was 39.6°C, HR 128 bpm and RR 42/min. Her mother gave a history of high fever in the last 4 days and severe cough. Chest examination revealed diminished air entry over the right lung, scattered fine consonating crepitations, and patch of bronchial breathing. The left lung was to totally normal. Chest x-ray revealed a total opacification of her right lung with no medication shift. CBC showed leukocytosis, shift to left and toxic granulations. Part II: All of the following are to be anticipated (expected) in that baby, except:","o":["Low O2 saturation.","High CRP.","Immediate mechanical ventilation","Immediate O2 and antibiotics."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"High fever, toxic appearance, focal bronchial breathing, leukocytosis with left shift and lobar consolidation support bacterial pneumonia.","more":"Explanation:\nHigh fever, toxic appearance, focal bronchial breathing, leukocytosis with left shift and lobar consolidation support bacterial pneumonia. Hypoxemia and raised CRP may be present. Oxygen and appropriate antibiotics are indicated according to severity. Mechanical ventilation is not automatic solely because there is severe pneumonia; it is reserved for respiratory failure or inability to maintain ventilation/oxygenation despite support.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nHigh fever, toxic appearance, focal bronchial breathing, leukocytosis with left shift and lobar consolidation support bacterial pneumonia.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q27 — Bacterial pneumonia with lobar opacification","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 103 · answer PDF p. 103 · original question Q27","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q28","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"All the following can be complicated by lung abscess, except:","o":["Lobar pneumonia.","Hydropneumothorax.","Tuberculosis.","Foreign body aspiration."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Lung abscess can complicate necrotizing or severe bacterial pneumonia, aspiration of infected material and airway obstruction from a foreign body; TB may also produce cavitary/abscess-like disease.","more":"Explanation:\nLung abscess can complicate necrotizing or severe bacterial pneumonia, aspiration of infected material and airway obstruction from a foreign body; TB may also produce cavitary/abscess-like disease. Hydropneumothorax is a pleural air-fluid collection and is not a usual predisposing cause of lung abscess. A lung abscess itself can rupture into the pleural space and cause pyopneumothorax, so direction of causality matters.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Lobar pneumonia.** — The other listed conditions can be associated with necrosis, aspiration or cavitary infection; hydropneumothorax is a distinct pleural-space process.\n- **b. Hydropneumothorax.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Hydropneumothorax is not a usual cause of lung abscess; an abscess can itself complicate into pleural disease.\n- **c. Tuberculosis.** — The other listed conditions can be associated with necrosis, aspiration or cavitary infection; hydropneumothorax is a distinct pleural-space process.\n- **d. Foreign body aspiration.** — The other listed conditions can be associated with necrosis, aspiration or cavitary infection; hydropneumothorax is a distinct pleural-space process.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nLung abscess can complicate necrotizing or severe bacterial pneumonia, aspiration of infected material and airway obstruction from a foreign body; TB may also produce cavitary/abscess-like disease.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q28 — Lung abscess: differential complication relationship","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 103 · answer PDF p. 103 · original question Q28","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q29","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Features","q":"In bronchiolitis:","o":["Coryzal symptoms usually follow the illness.","Wheezing is often but not always present.","Bronchiolitis obliterans is the most common complication.","Usually caused by parainfluenza virus."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Bronchiolitis typically begins with upper respiratory catarrh followed by cough, tachypnea, crackles and variable wheeze.","more":"Explanation:\nBronchiolitis typically begins with upper respiratory catarrh followed by cough, tachypnea, crackles and variable wheeze. Not every infant wheezes. Bronchiolitis obliterans is an uncommon chronic complication, not the most common complication; RSV is the leading cause in many settings, though other viruses can cause it.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Coryzal symptoms usually follow the illness.** — Coryza generally precedes lower airway symptoms; bronchiolitis obliterans is uncommon; parainfluenza is not the commonest cause.\n- **b. Wheezing is often but not always present.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Wheezing is often, but not invariably, present in bronchiolitis.\n- **c. Bronchiolitis obliterans is the most common complication.** — Coryza generally precedes lower airway symptoms; bronchiolitis obliterans is uncommon; parainfluenza is not the commonest cause.\n- **d. Usually caused by parainfluenza virus.** — Coryza generally precedes lower airway symptoms; bronchiolitis obliterans is uncommon; parainfluenza is not the commonest cause.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nBronchiolitis typically begins with upper respiratory catarrh followed by cough, tachypnea, crackles and variable wheeze.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q29 — Wheezing is common but not universal in bronchiolitis","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 103 · answer PDF p. 103 · original question Q29","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q3","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"The dose of rifampicin (an anti-tuberculous drug) is:","o":["6-8 mg/kg/day.","10-20 mg/kg/day.","20-40 mg/kg/day.","40-60 mg/kg/day."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"The source range of 10–20 mg/kg/day contains the commonly recommended daily pediatric rifampicin dose (often 15–20 mg/kg, subject to maximum dose and national protocol).","more":"Explanation:\nThe source range of 10–20 mg/kg/day contains the commonly recommended daily pediatric rifampicin dose (often 15–20 mg/kg, subject to maximum dose and national protocol). TB drugs are prescribed as a complete multidrug regimen, not as isolated monotherapy. Confirm current local guidance, formulation, weight band, interactions and maximum dose.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. 6-8 mg/kg/day.** — 6–8 mg/kg/day is generally below common pediatric daily dosing; 20–40 and 40–60 mg/kg/day are substantially higher than usual rifampicin dosing.\n- **b. 10-20 mg/kg/day.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. The 10–20 mg/kg/day option includes the usual pediatric daily range; use a complete TB regimen and current local dosing table.\n- **c. 20-40 mg/kg/day.** — 6–8 mg/kg/day is generally below common pediatric daily dosing; 20–40 and 40–60 mg/kg/day are substantially higher than usual rifampicin dosing.\n- **d. 40-60 mg/kg/day.** — 6–8 mg/kg/day is generally below common pediatric daily dosing; 20–40 and 40–60 mg/kg/day are substantially higher than usual rifampicin dosing.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nRifampicin has important drug interactions and can discolor body fluids orange-red.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q3 — Pediatric rifampicin dose","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 97 · answer PDF p. 97 · original question Q3","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q30","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"Which of the following is true about bronchiolitis?","o":["It is one of the atopic disorders.","Most cases are caused by adenovirus.","Oxygen therapy is the main line of management.","Recurrence is very common."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Bronchiolitis management is supportive.","more":"Explanation:\nBronchiolitis management is supportive. Oxygen is given when hypoxemia meets the local threshold or there are other clinical indications; it is not required for every infant. Hydration/feeding support, nasal clearance and monitoring are also central. The question calls oxygen “the main line of management” without specifying oxygen saturation or severity, so the source answer c is overgeneralized, although oxygen is an important intervention in hypoxemic bronchiolitis.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. It is one of the atopic disorders.** — Adenovirus is not the commonest cause; bronchiolitis is viral, not an atopic disorder; recurrence is not a defining feature.\n- **b. Most cases are caused by adenovirus.** — Adenovirus is not the commonest cause; bronchiolitis is viral, not an atopic disorder; recurrence is not a defining feature.\n- **c. Oxygen therapy is the main line of management.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Supportive care is the mainstay; supplemental oxygen is used when clinically indicated, not automatically in every case.\n- **d. Recurrence is very common.** — Adenovirus is not the commonest cause; bronchiolitis is viral, not an atopic disorder; recurrence is not a defining feature.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nBronchiolitis management is supportive.\n\nReview note:\nNeeds Review because hydration/feeding and supportive care are omitted and oxygen is indicated selectively.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q30 — Bronchiolitis management: oxygen is conditional, not universal first-line therapy","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 103 · answer PDF p. 103 · original question Q30","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q31-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Investigation","q":"Two-year-old child presented to ER acute onset of respiratory distress which followed recent change of residence. His mother said that this happened after she left him playing in the garden with his toys. On examination, there were right basal lung wheezes and intercostal retractions. The left side of his chest was free: Part I: What's the most likely diagnosis:","o":["Bronchial asthma.","Foreign body inhalation.","Immune deficiency.","Bronchiectasis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Sudden respiratory symptoms in a previously well toddler, a plausible unsupervised-play episode and unilateral focal wheeze strongly suggest foreign-body aspiration.","more":"Explanation:\nSudden respiratory symptoms in a previously well toddler, a plausible unsupervised-play episode and unilateral focal wheeze strongly suggest foreign-body aspiration. A normal chest radiograph does not exclude it. Rigid bronchoscopy is both diagnostic and therapeutic for a suspected aspirated airway foreign body; pulmonary function tests, sputum testing and serum immunoglobulins do not answer the acute question.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nSudden respiratory symptoms in a previously well toddler, a plausible unsupervised-play episode and unilateral focal wheeze strongly suggest foreign-body aspiration.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q31 — Unilateral wheeze after unsupervised play: inhaled foreign body","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 103 · answer PDF p. 104 · original question Q31","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q31-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Investigation","q":"Two-year-old child presented to ER acute onset of respiratory distress which followed recent change of residence. His mother said that this happened after she left him playing in the garden with his toys. On examination, there were right basal lung wheezes and intercostal retractions. The left side of his chest was free: Part II: Investigation will be most helpful:","o":["Pulmonary function test.","Sputum examination.","Serum immunoglobulins.","Bronchoscopy."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Sudden respiratory symptoms in a previously well toddler, a plausible unsupervised-play episode and unilateral focal wheeze strongly suggest foreign-body aspiration.","more":"Explanation:\nSudden respiratory symptoms in a previously well toddler, a plausible unsupervised-play episode and unilateral focal wheeze strongly suggest foreign-body aspiration. A normal chest radiograph does not exclude it. Rigid bronchoscopy is both diagnostic and therapeutic for a suspected aspirated airway foreign body; pulmonary function tests, sputum testing and serum immunoglobulins do not answer the acute question.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nSudden respiratory symptoms in a previously well toddler, a plausible unsupervised-play episode and unilateral focal wheeze strongly suggest foreign-body aspiration.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q31 — Unilateral wheeze after unsupervised play: inhaled foreign body","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 103 · answer PDF p. 104 · original question Q31","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q32","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"Long term medication for asthma:","o":["Theophylline.","Ipratropium.","Montelukast.","Terbutaline."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Montelukast is a leukotriene receptor antagonist used as a controller option in selected children, although inhaled corticosteroid-containing therapy is generally preferred for most children with persistent asthma.","more":"Explanation:\nMontelukast is a leukotriene receptor antagonist used as a controller option in selected children, although inhaled corticosteroid-containing therapy is generally preferred for most children with persistent asthma. Theophylline is not preferred because of its narrow therapeutic index and adverse effects; ipratropium is primarily an acute add-on bronchodilator; terbutaline is a beta-agonist reliever rather than routine long-term controller.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Theophylline.** — Theophylline is not preferred routine maintenance therapy; ipratropium is mainly short-term add-on treatment; terbutaline is a bronchodilator/reliever.\n- **b. Ipratropium.** — Theophylline is not preferred routine maintenance therapy; ipratropium is mainly short-term add-on treatment; terbutaline is a bronchodilator/reliever.\n- **c. Montelukast.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Montelukast is the listed long-term controller option, though not usually preferred over ICS-containing treatment.\n- **d. Terbutaline.** — Theophylline is not preferred routine maintenance therapy; ipratropium is mainly short-term add-on treatment; terbutaline is a bronchodilator/reliever.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nMontelukast is a leukotriene receptor antagonist used as a controller option in selected children, although inhaled corticosteroid-containing therapy is generally preferred for most children with persistent asthma.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q32 — Montelukast is a long-term asthma controller among the listed options","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 104 · answer PDF p. 104 · original question Q32","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q33","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"All the following are complicated by lung abscess, except:","o":["Acute bronchiolitis.","Amoebiasis.","Staph pneumonia.","Foreign body aspiration."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Lung abscess may follow severe bacterial infection (including staphylococcal pneumonia), aspiration/airway obstruction and selected infections such as amoebiasis.","more":"Explanation:\nLung abscess may follow severe bacterial infection (including staphylococcal pneumonia), aspiration/airway obstruction and selected infections such as amoebiasis. Acute bronchiolitis is a viral bronchiolar illness and is not a usual cause of a focal suppurative parenchymal abscess.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Acute bronchiolitis.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Acute bronchiolitis is the exception.\n- **b. Amoebiasis.** — Staphylococcal pneumonia and aspirated foreign bodies can produce necrotizing infection/obstruction; amoebic infection can rarely produce pulmonary abscess.\n- **c. Staph pneumonia.** — Staphylococcal pneumonia and aspirated foreign bodies can produce necrotizing infection/obstruction; amoebic infection can rarely produce pulmonary abscess.\n- **d. Foreign body aspiration.** — Staphylococcal pneumonia and aspirated foreign bodies can produce necrotizing infection/obstruction; amoebic infection can rarely produce pulmonary abscess.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nLung abscess may follow severe bacterial infection (including staphylococcal pneumonia), aspiration/airway obstruction and selected infections such as amoebiasis.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q33 — Acute bronchiolitis is not a usual cause of lung abscess","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 104 · answer PDF p. 104 · original question Q33","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q34","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"The most likely causative agent of acute bronchiolitis is:","o":["Adenovirus.","Para-influenza virus.","Epstein-Barr virus.","Respiratory syncytial virus."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"RSV is the leading cause of bronchiolitis in infants and young children.","more":"Explanation:\nRSV is the leading cause of bronchiolitis in infants and young children. Other viruses include rhinovirus, human metapneumovirus, parainfluenza, influenza and adenovirus. The diagnosis is usually clinical and management supportive.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Adenovirus.** — Adenovirus, parainfluenza and EBV may cause respiratory illness but are not the typical most common bronchiolitis pathogen.\n- **b. Para-influenza virus.** — Adenovirus, parainfluenza and EBV may cause respiratory illness but are not the typical most common bronchiolitis pathogen.\n- **c. Epstein-Barr virus.** — Adenovirus, parainfluenza and EBV may cause respiratory illness but are not the typical most common bronchiolitis pathogen.\n- **d. Respiratory syncytial virus.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) is the commonest classic cause.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nRSV is the leading cause of bronchiolitis in infants and young children.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q34 — Respiratory syncytial virus is the commonest cause of acute bronchiolitis","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 104 · answer PDF p. 104 · original question Q34","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q35","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Bronchial asthma characterized by:","o":["Common atopic disease of childhood.","Recurrence is very rare.","Low degree of reversibility of obstructive process.","Bandemia is a constant feature in peripheral blood film."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Asthma is a heterogeneous chronic inflammatory airway disease characterized by variable respiratory symptoms and variable expiratory airflow limitation.","more":"Explanation:\nAsthma is a heterogeneous chronic inflammatory airway disease characterized by variable respiratory symptoms and variable expiratory airflow limitation. Atopy is common in childhood allergic asthma, but not every child has an atopic phenotype. Airflow limitation is variable and often reversible, and bandemia is not a constant blood-film feature.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Common atopic disease of childhood.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Asthma is a common atopic disease of childhood.\n- **b. Recurrence is very rare.** — Recurrence is common; airflow limitation is variable/reversible rather than fixed low-reversibility by definition; bandemia is not a diagnostic constant.\n- **c. Low degree of reversibility of obstructive process.** — Recurrence is common; airflow limitation is variable/reversible rather than fixed low-reversibility by definition; bandemia is not a diagnostic constant.\n- **d. Bandemia is a constant feature in peripheral blood film.** — Recurrence is common; airflow limitation is variable/reversible rather than fixed low-reversibility by definition; bandemia is not a diagnostic constant.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAsthma is a heterogeneous chronic inflammatory airway disease characterized by variable respiratory symptoms and variable expiratory airflow limitation.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q35 — Childhood asthma is a common atopic disease","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 104 · answer PDF p. 104 · original question Q35","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q36","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 2-year-old boy is seen because of sudden onset shortness of breath and cough, he had an upper respiratory infection 4 days ago. Earlier in the day, he was playing with peanuts. His immunization is up to date examination revealed respiratory distress with inspiratory stridor auscultation showed tachycardia & decrease breath sounds in right lower base. Diagnosis:","o":["Bronchial asthma.","Viral croup.","Acute epiglottitis.","Foreign body aspiration."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"A toddler playing with peanuts who develops sudden cough/dyspnea, inspiratory noise and reduced unilateral air entry should be assumed to have aspirated a foreign body until excluded.","more":"Explanation:\nA toddler playing with peanuts who develops sudden cough/dyspnea, inspiratory noise and reduced unilateral air entry should be assumed to have aspirated a foreign body until excluded. Partial obstruction can cause stridor or wheeze and unilateral air trapping; complete obstruction can rapidly become fatal. The airway needs urgent specialist assessment and bronchoscopy when suspected.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Bronchial asthma.** — Asthma tends to cause diffuse wheeze; croup causes barking cough/stridor with a more typical viral prodrome; epiglottitis usually causes toxic appearance, drooling and tripod posture.\n- **b. Viral croup.** — Asthma tends to cause diffuse wheeze; croup causes barking cough/stridor with a more typical viral prodrome; epiglottitis usually causes toxic appearance, drooling and tripod posture.\n- **c. Acute epiglottitis.** — Asthma tends to cause diffuse wheeze; croup causes barking cough/stridor with a more typical viral prodrome; epiglottitis usually causes toxic appearance, drooling and tripod posture.\n- **d. Foreign body aspiration.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Foreign-body aspiration is the best diagnosis.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA toddler playing with peanuts who develops sudden cough/dyspnea, inspiratory noise and reduced unilateral air entry should be assumed to have aspirated a foreign body until excluded.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q36 — Sudden onset unilateral respiratory signs after peanut play: foreign-body aspiration","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 104 · answer PDF p. 104 · original question Q36","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q37","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"Serious complications of staphylococcal pneumonia include all the following, except:","o":["Lung abscess.","Bronchitis.","Empyema.","Lung collapse."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Staphylococcal pneumonia can cause necrotizing pneumonia, lung abscess, empyema, pneumatoceles and occasionally pneumothorax or lung collapse. “Bronchitis” is not usually classified as a serious suppurative complication of staphylococcal pneumonia.","more":"Explanation:\nStaphylococcal pneumonia can cause necrotizing pneumonia, lung abscess, empyema, pneumatoceles and occasionally pneumothorax or lung collapse. “Bronchitis” is not usually classified as a serious suppurative complication of staphylococcal pneumonia.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Lung abscess.** — Abscess, empyema and collapse are recognized severe pulmonary complications of destructive staphylococcal infection.\n- **b. Bronchitis.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Bronchitis is the exception.\n- **c. Empyema.** — Abscess, empyema and collapse are recognized severe pulmonary complications of destructive staphylococcal infection.\n- **d. Lung collapse.** — Abscess, empyema and collapse are recognized severe pulmonary complications of destructive staphylococcal infection.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nStaphylococcal pneumonia can cause necrotizing pneumonia, lung abscess, empyema, pneumatoceles and occasionally pneumothorax or lung collapse. “Bronchitis” is not usually classified as a serious suppurative complication of staphylococcal pneumonia.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q37 — Bronchitis is not a major serious complication of staphylococcal pneumonia","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 105 · answer PDF p. 105 · original question Q37","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q38","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"Paroxysmal stage of pertussis persists for:","o":["2 weeks.","3weeks.","4 weeks.","6 weeks."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Pertussis progresses through catarrhal, paroxysmal and convalescent stages.","more":"Explanation:\nPertussis progresses through catarrhal, paroxysmal and convalescent stages. The paroxysmal stage is characterized by recurrent bouts of rapid cough, inspiratory whoop, post-tussive vomiting, cyanosis and exhaustion. Its duration is variable; classic teaching commonly gives about 2–6 weeks, with a plateau of attacks over 2–3 weeks before gradual decline. Four weeks is a plausible textbook estimate among the options, not a fixed duration.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. 2 weeks.** — Two, three, four and six weeks overlap the variable clinical course; no single duration applies to every child.\n- **b. 3weeks.** — Two, three, four and six weeks overlap the variable clinical course; no single duration applies to every child.\n- **c. 4 weeks.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. The source’s four-week option is a plausible approximate teaching duration, but the stage varies considerably.\n- **d. 6 weeks.** — Two, three, four and six weeks overlap the variable clinical course; no single duration applies to every child.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nIn infants, apnea may occur with little or no characteristic whoop; they are at highest risk of severe disease.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q38 — Pertussis paroxysmal stage: duration varies, classically several weeks","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 105 · answer PDF p. 105 · original question Q38","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q39-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"9-month-old male is brought to clinic with history of episodes of paroxysms of cough since 1 month. Each paroxysm of cough is followed by vomiting and usually associated with inspiratory characteristic sound and intense congestion of the face. Examination of the chest is unremarkable and chest x-ray is normal. Part I: What is the most likely diagnosis:","o":["Tuberculosis.","Whooping cough.","Bronchial asthma.","Viral croup."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Paroxysmal cough, post-tussive vomiting and an inspiratory whoop are classic pertussis features.","more":"Explanation:\nParoxysmal cough, post-tussive vomiting and an inspiratory whoop are classic pertussis features. Lymphocytosis is classically associated with pertussis, while pneumothorax is a recognized complication of forceful coughing. Steroids and montelukast are not routine specific treatments. Early macrolide therapy is most useful for reducing symptoms and transmission when started early; late treatment may not shorten the paroxysmal cough.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nParoxysmal cough, post-tussive vomiting and an inspiratory whoop are classic pertussis features.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q39 — Pertussis with paroxysmal cough and post-tussive vomiting","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 105 · answer PDF p. 105 · original question Q39","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q39-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"9-month-old male is brought to clinic with history of episodes of paroxysms of cough since 1 month. Each paroxysm of cough is followed by vomiting and usually associated with inspiratory characteristic sound and intense congestion of the face. Examination of the chest is unremarkable and chest x-ray is normal. Part II: One of the following is true about diagnosis:","o":["Lymphopenia is common.","Steroids are usually indicated.","Pneumothorax may occur.","Montelukast is a useful therapy."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Paroxysmal cough, post-tussive vomiting and an inspiratory whoop are classic pertussis features.","more":"Explanation:\nParoxysmal cough, post-tussive vomiting and an inspiratory whoop are classic pertussis features. Lymphocytosis is classically associated with pertussis, while pneumothorax is a recognized complication of forceful coughing. Steroids and montelukast are not routine specific treatments. Early macrolide therapy is most useful for reducing symptoms and transmission when started early; late treatment may not shorten the paroxysmal cough.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nParoxysmal cough, post-tussive vomiting and an inspiratory whoop are classic pertussis features.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q39 — Pertussis with paroxysmal cough and post-tussive vomiting","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 105 · answer PDF p. 105 · original question Q39","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q4","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"In which grade of respiratory distress grunting is heard:","o":["Grade l.","Grade 2.","Grade 3.","Grade 4."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Grunting is an expiratory maneuver that helps generate positive end-expiratory pressure and maintain alveolar recruitment; it is a sign of significant respiratory distress.","more":"Explanation:\nGrunting is an expiratory maneuver that helps generate positive end-expiratory pressure and maintain alveolar recruitment; it is a sign of significant respiratory distress. The source maps it to grade 3, but the grading scale is not named in the stem and several pediatric distress scales exist. Clinical interpretation should prioritize work of breathing, oxygenation, mental status and fatigue rather than an unreferenced ordinal grade.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Grade l.** — The key clinical concept is the significance of grunting, not memorizing an unsupported grade label.\n- **b. Grade 2.** — The key clinical concept is the significance of grunting, not memorizing an unsupported grade label.\n- **c. Grade 3.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Grunting signals clinically important respiratory distress; the exact grade depends on the named scale.\n- **d. Grade 4.** — The key clinical concept is the significance of grunting, not memorizing an unsupported grade label.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nGrunting is an expiratory maneuver that helps generate positive end-expiratory pressure and maintain alveolar recruitment; it is a sign of significant respiratory distress.\n\nReview note:\nVerify which respiratory-distress grading scale the lecturer uses before treating “grade 3” as a universal fact.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q4 — Respiratory distress grading: grunting assigned to grade 3 in the source","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 97 · answer PDF p. 97 · original question Q4","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"","aoAltSource":"PDF p. 57 · Printed p. 56 · Original question number: Q20"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q40","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"Commonest cause of bacterial pneumonia in infancy is:","o":["H. influenzae.","Streptococcus.","Staphylococcus.","Pneumococcus."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Streptococcus pneumoniae remains a major bacterial cause of community-acquired pneumonia in infants and children.","more":"Explanation:\nStreptococcus pneumoniae remains a major bacterial cause of community-acquired pneumonia in infants and children. Haemophilus influenzae and Staphylococcus aureus are also important in selected settings and vaccination contexts; pathogen distribution varies by age and epidemiology.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. H. influenzae.** — H. influenzae and staphylococci can cause disease but are not generally the single most common bacterial cause in this classic teaching context.\n- **b. Streptococcus.** — H. influenzae and staphylococci can cause disease but are not generally the single most common bacterial cause in this classic teaching context.\n- **c. Staphylococcus.** — H. influenzae and staphylococci can cause disease but are not generally the single most common bacterial cause in this classic teaching context.\n- **d. Pneumococcus.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Pneumococcus is a leading common bacterial cause.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nStreptococcus pneumoniae remains a major bacterial cause of community-acquired pneumonia in infants and children.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q40 — Pneumococcus is a leading bacterial cause of pneumonia in infancy","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 105 · answer PDF p. 105 · original question Q40","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q41","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"The sound that is heard in the chest of asthmatic child is:","o":["Croup.","Snoring.","Wheezing.","Murmur."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Wheeze is a musical continuous sound, usually expiratory, caused by airflow through narrowed intrathoracic airways.","more":"Explanation:\nWheeze is a musical continuous sound, usually expiratory, caused by airflow through narrowed intrathoracic airways. It is common in asthma, although severe obstruction may produce a quiet/silent chest rather than audible wheeze.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Croup.** — Croup is a syndrome with inspiratory stridor; snoring is an upper-airway sleep sound; murmur is a cardiac auscultatory finding.\n- **b. Snoring.** — Croup is a syndrome with inspiratory stridor; snoring is an upper-airway sleep sound; murmur is a cardiac auscultatory finding.\n- **c. Wheezing.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Wheezing is the expected sound.\n- **d. Murmur.** — Croup is a syndrome with inspiratory stridor; snoring is an upper-airway sleep sound; murmur is a cardiac auscultatory finding.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nWheeze is a musical continuous sound, usually expiratory, caused by airflow through narrowed intrathoracic airways.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q41 — Wheezing is the characteristic auscultatory sound in asthma","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 105 · answer PDF p. 105 · original question Q41","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q42","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Complications","q":"All the following are known complications of pertussis, except:","o":["Otitis media.","Convulsions.","Peripheral neuritis.","Rectal prolapse."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Pertussis can cause otitis media, seizures/encephalopathy from hypoxia or toxin effects, apnea, pneumonia, pneumothorax and rectal prolapse from severe coughing.","more":"Explanation:\nPertussis can cause otitis media, seizures/encephalopathy from hypoxia or toxin effects, apnea, pneumonia, pneumothorax and rectal prolapse from severe coughing. Peripheral neuritis is not a standard recognized common complication in the usual pediatric complication lists.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Otitis media.** — Otitis media, seizures and rectal prolapse are recognized complications, particularly with severe/prolonged paroxysms.\n- **b. Convulsions.** — Otitis media, seizures and rectal prolapse are recognized complications, particularly with severe/prolonged paroxysms.\n- **c. Peripheral neuritis.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Peripheral neuritis is the exception.\n- **d. Rectal prolapse.** — Otitis media, seizures and rectal prolapse are recognized complications, particularly with severe/prolonged paroxysms.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nPertussis can cause otitis media, seizures/encephalopathy from hypoxia or toxin effects, apnea, pneumonia, pneumothorax and rectal prolapse from severe coughing.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q42 — Peripheral neuritis is not a typical pertussis complication","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 105 · answer PDF p. 106 · original question Q42","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q43-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 4-year-old girl presents with a 3-day history of fever, cough, and respiratory distress. This has progressively worsened. She has previously been healthy. On examination, she has fever of 39.8°C, and respiratory rate of 45/minutes with moderate recession. Oxygen saturations are 89% in air. She has crackles at right base. What is the most likely diagnosis:","o":["Viral pneumonia.","Whooping cough.","Bacterial pneumonia.","Bronchial asthma."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Fever, progressive respiratory distress, hypoxemia and focal crackles suggest pneumonia; the source pattern with bacterial fever and localized crackles supports bacterial disease.","more":"Explanation:\nFever, progressive respiratory distress, hypoxemia and focal crackles suggest pneumonia; the source pattern with bacterial fever and localized crackles supports bacterial disease. Lobar consolidation is expected, and inflammatory markers may rise. Leukopenia, hyperinflation and a negative CRP are not the expected combination in this vignette.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nFever, progressive respiratory distress, hypoxemia and focal crackles suggest pneumonia; the source pattern with bacterial fever and localized crackles supports bacterial disease.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q43 — Bacterial pneumonia with focal consolidation and hypoxemia","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 106 · answer PDF p. 106 · original question Q43","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q43-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 4-year-old girl presents with a 3-day history of fever, cough, and respiratory distress. This has progressively worsened. She has previously been healthy. On examination, she has fever of 39.8°C, and respiratory rate of 45/minutes with moderate recession. Oxygen saturations are 89% in air. She has crackles at right base. What is the most likely diagnosis:","o":["Viral pneumonia.","Whooping cough.","Bacterial pneumonia.","Bronchial asthma."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Fever, progressive respiratory distress, hypoxemia and focal crackles suggest pneumonia; the source pattern with bacterial fever and localized crackles supports bacterial disease.","more":"Explanation:\nFever, progressive respiratory distress, hypoxemia and focal crackles suggest pneumonia; the source pattern with bacterial fever and localized crackles supports bacterial disease. Lobar consolidation is expected, and inflammatory markers may rise. Leukopenia, hyperinflation and a negative CRP are not the expected combination in this vignette.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nFever, progressive respiratory distress, hypoxemia and focal crackles suggest pneumonia; the source pattern with bacterial fever and localized crackles supports bacterial disease.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q43 — Bacterial pneumonia with focal consolidation and hypoxemia","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 106 · answer PDF p. 106 · original question Q43","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q44-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A mother of a previously healthy 3-year old male complains of cough and wheezes. The boy had been playing with a small toy. During examination, the right side of the heart shows hyperresonance, diminished vocal resonance and poor air entry. The most probable diagnosis:","o":["Foreign body aspiration.","Bronchial asthma.","Lobar pneumonia.","Acute bronchitis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"A previously healthy child with sudden cough/wheeze after playing with a toy and unilateral hyperresonance, reduced vocal resonance and poor air entry has a classic ball-valve foreign-body pattern with air trapping.","more":"Explanation:\nA previously healthy child with sudden cough/wheeze after playing with a toy and unilateral hyperresonance, reduced vocal resonance and poor air entry has a classic ball-valve foreign-body pattern with air trapping. The abrupt onset and local chest findings together strongly support aspiration. Bronchoscopy is required when clinical suspicion is high, even if initial imaging is unrevealing.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA previously healthy child with sudden cough/wheeze after playing with a toy and unilateral hyperresonance, reduced vocal resonance and poor air entry has a classic ball-valve foreign-body pattern with air trapping.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q44 — Foreign-body aspiration: healthy child with unilateral hyperinflation","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 106 · answer PDF p. 106 · original question Q44","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q44-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A mother of a previously healthy 3-year old male complains of cough and wheezes. The boy had been playing with a small toy. During examination, the right side of the heart shows hyperresonance, diminished vocal resonance and poor air entry. The most probable diagnosis:","o":["Foreign body aspiration.","Bronchial asthma.","Lobar pneumonia.","Acute bronchitis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"A previously healthy child with sudden cough/wheeze after playing with a toy and unilateral hyperresonance, reduced vocal resonance and poor air entry has a classic ball-valve foreign-body pattern with air trapping.","more":"Explanation:\nA previously healthy child with sudden cough/wheeze after playing with a toy and unilateral hyperresonance, reduced vocal resonance and poor air entry has a classic ball-valve foreign-body pattern with air trapping. The abrupt onset and local chest findings together strongly support aspiration. Bronchoscopy is required when clinical suspicion is high, even if initial imaging is unrevealing.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA previously healthy child with sudden cough/wheeze after playing with a toy and unilateral hyperresonance, reduced vocal resonance and poor air entry has a classic ball-valve foreign-body pattern with air trapping.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q44 — Foreign-body aspiration: healthy child with unilateral hyperinflation","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 106 · answer PDF p. 106 · original question Q44","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q45-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 6-year-old patient with severe pneumonia is being treated with intravenous cefuroxime and is doing well until day 3 of hospitalization, when he develops a temperature of 39.2°C and complains of unilateral pleuritic chest pain and associated shortness of breath. Part I: The most likely diagnosis is:","o":["Viral myocarditis.","Pleural effusion.","Foreign body aspiration.","Diaphragmatic hernia."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"New fever, unilateral pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea after several days of pneumonia suggest a parapneumonic effusion or evolving empyema.","more":"Explanation:\nNew fever, unilateral pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea after several days of pneumonia suggest a parapneumonic effusion or evolving empyema. Chest imaging is the next step to identify and assess the effusion; ultrasound can characterize the fluid. Further management depends on size, respiratory compromise, complexity and purulence.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nNew fever, unilateral pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea after several days of pneumonia suggest a parapneumonic effusion or evolving empyema.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q45 — Parapneumonic pleural effusion after pneumonia","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 106 · answer PDF p. 107 · original question Q45","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q45-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 6-year-old patient with severe pneumonia is being treated with intravenous cefuroxime and is doing well until day 3 of hospitalization, when he develops a temperature of 39.2°C and complains of unilateral pleuritic chest pain and associated shortness of breath. Part II: The next step of management is:","o":["Obtain x-ray of the chest.","Perform an echocardiogram.","Arrange for bronchoscopy.","Ask for surgical consultation."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"New fever, unilateral pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea after several days of pneumonia suggest a parapneumonic effusion or evolving empyema.","more":"Explanation:\nNew fever, unilateral pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea after several days of pneumonia suggest a parapneumonic effusion or evolving empyema. Chest imaging is the next step to identify and assess the effusion; ultrasound can characterize the fluid. Further management depends on size, respiratory compromise, complexity and purulence.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nNew fever, unilateral pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea after several days of pneumonia suggest a parapneumonic effusion or evolving empyema.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q45 — Parapneumonic pleural effusion after pneumonia","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 106 · answer PDF p. 107 · original question Q45","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q46","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"Suppurative lung diseases include all except:","o":["Bronchiectasis.","Lung abscess.","TB.","Empyema."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"The classic suppurative lung disease group includes lung abscess, bronchiectasis and empyema.","more":"Explanation:\nThe classic suppurative lung disease group includes lung abscess, bronchiectasis and empyema. TB can cause cavitation and chronic destructive lung disease, but it is not conventionally included in this narrow triad of suppurative diseases used by the source note.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Bronchiectasis.** — Bronchiectasis, lung abscess and empyema are the listed suppurative entities.\n- **b. Lung abscess.** — Bronchiectasis, lung abscess and empyema are the listed suppurative entities.\n- **c. TB.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. TB is the exception in the source’s classification list.\n- **d. Empyema.** — Bronchiectasis, lung abscess and empyema are the listed suppurative entities.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe classic suppurative lung disease group includes lung abscess, bronchiectasis and empyema.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q46 — Tuberculosis is not classically grouped among suppurative lung diseases","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 107 · answer PDF p. 107 · original question Q46","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q47","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 16-week-old infant is brought to clinic because of cough, labored breathing, wheezing, and fever of two days' duration following a runny nose. The baby was delivered prematurely at 28 weeks. Chest examination reveals tachypnea, intercostal retractions, nasal flaring, hyperresonance to percussion, expiratory wheezing, and prolonged expiratory phase. What is the most likely diagnosis:","o":["Asthma.","Heart failure.","Pneumonia.","Bronchiolitis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"A 16-week-old infant with coryza followed by cough, wheezing, tachypnea, retractions, nasal flaring and prolonged expiration has bronchiolitis.","more":"Explanation:\nA 16-week-old infant with coryza followed by cough, wheezing, tachypnea, retractions, nasal flaring and prolonged expiration has bronchiolitis. Prematurity increases the risk of severe disease. Management is supportive, with oxygen for hypoxemia, hydration/feeding assistance and monitoring for apnea or exhaustion.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Asthma.** — Asthma can wheeze but is less likely in a first acute viral illness at this age; pneumonia and heart failure have different associated findings and patterns.\n- **b. Heart failure.** — Asthma can wheeze but is less likely in a first acute viral illness at this age; pneumonia and heart failure have different associated findings and patterns.\n- **c. Pneumonia.** — Asthma can wheeze but is less likely in a first acute viral illness at this age; pneumonia and heart failure have different associated findings and patterns.\n- **d. Bronchiolitis.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Acute bronchiolitis is most likely.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA 16-week-old infant with coryza followed by cough, wheezing, tachypnea, retractions, nasal flaring and prolonged expiration has bronchiolitis.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q47 — Bronchiolitis in a young premature infant","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 107 · answer PDF p. 107 · original question Q47","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q48-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 6-year-old girl has had a dry cough without sputum for 2 months. The cough is worse after exercise and at night. Family history revealed that the parents have eczema. On physical examination, a wheeze in both lung fields is detected. Part I: What is the most likely diagnosis:","o":["Bronchial asthma.","Pertussis.","Bronchiectasis.","Interstitial pneumonia."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Recurrent dry cough, symptoms worse at night and after exercise, parental eczema and bilateral wheeze support asthma.","more":"Explanation:\nRecurrent dry cough, symptoms worse at night and after exercise, parental eczema and bilateral wheeze support asthma. A good response to bronchodilator supports variable airflow limitation. Clubbing, fever, elevated ESR and absolute lymphocytosis are not expected diagnostic features of uncomplicated asthma.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nRecurrent dry cough, symptoms worse at night and after exercise, parental eczema and bilateral wheeze support asthma.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q48 — Exercise- and night-related cough with atopy suggests asthma","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 107 · answer PDF p. 107 · original question Q48","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q48-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 6-year-old girl has had a dry cough without sputum for 2 months. The cough is worse after exercise and at night. Family history revealed that the parents have eczema. On physical examination, a wheeze in both lung fields is detected. Part II: One of the following is an expected finding:","o":["Clubbing of fingers.","Absolute lymphocytosis","High fever and elevated ESR.","Good response to bronchodilators."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Recurrent dry cough, symptoms worse at night and after exercise, parental eczema and bilateral wheeze support asthma.","more":"Explanation:\nRecurrent dry cough, symptoms worse at night and after exercise, parental eczema and bilateral wheeze support asthma. A good response to bronchodilator supports variable airflow limitation. Clubbing, fever, elevated ESR and absolute lymphocytosis are not expected diagnostic features of uncomplicated asthma.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nRecurrent dry cough, symptoms worse at night and after exercise, parental eczema and bilateral wheeze support asthma.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q48 — Exercise- and night-related cough with atopy suggests asthma","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 107 · answer PDF p. 107 · original question Q48","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q49","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Complications","q":"All the following are common complications of pertussis, except:","o":["Secondary bronchopneumonia.","Aplastic anemia.","Pneumothorax.","Apnea in small infants."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Pertussis complications include secondary pneumonia, apnea in young infants and pneumothorax from severe coughing.","more":"Explanation:\nPertussis complications include secondary pneumonia, apnea in young infants and pneumothorax from severe coughing. Aplastic anemia is not a standard common complication. The source answer b is therefore appropriate.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Secondary bronchopneumonia.** — Pneumonia, pneumothorax and apnea are recognized complications of severe pertussis.\n- **b. Aplastic anemia.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Aplastic anemia is the exception.\n- **c. Pneumothorax.** — Pneumonia, pneumothorax and apnea are recognized complications of severe pertussis.\n- **d. Apnea in small infants.** — Pneumonia, pneumothorax and apnea are recognized complications of severe pertussis.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nPertussis complications include secondary pneumonia, apnea in young infants and pneumothorax from severe coughing.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q49 — Aplastic anemia is not a common complication of pertussis","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 107 · answer PDF p. 107 · original question Q49","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q5-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 6-year-old girl presented to ER with severe irritability and nonstop crying of 2 hours duration. The mother said that her girl had a runny nose since 5 days. On examination, the girl was febrile (temperature 38.7°C), a yellowish discharge was seen coming from the right ear and the left tympanic membrane was congested and bulging. Part I: The most likely diagnosis is:","o":["Acute otitis externa.","Acute otitis media.","Acute mastoiditis.","Acute labyrinthitis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Fever, otalgia/irritability, otorrhea and a congested, bulging tympanic membrane strongly support acute otitis media (AOM).","more":"Explanation:\nFever, otalgia/irritability, otorrhea and a congested, bulging tympanic membrane strongly support acute otitis media (AOM). A bulging tympanic membrane is a key diagnostic feature; purulent otorrhea may occur after spontaneous perforation. Streptococcus pneumoniae is an important common bacterial cause, alongside non-typeable Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis. Otitis externa causes canal inflammation and pain with tragal/pinna movement; mastoiditis causes postauricular tenderness/swelling and displaced pinna.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe diagnosis depends on middle-ear effusion and acute inflammation; redness alone is not enough.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q5 — Acute otitis media after an upper respiratory infection","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 97 · answer PDF p. 98 · original question Q5","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q5-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 6-year-old girl presented to ER with severe irritability and nonstop crying of 2 hours duration. The mother said that her girl had a runny nose since 5 days. On examination, the girl was febrile (temperature 38.7°C), a yellowish discharge was seen coming from the right ear and the left tympanic membrane was congested and bulging. Part II: The commonest bacteria that causes this condition is:","o":["Staphylococcus aureus.","Streptococcus pneumoniae.","Pseudomonas aeruginosa.","Group A beta hemolytic streptococcus."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Fever, otalgia/irritability, otorrhea and a congested, bulging tympanic membrane strongly support acute otitis media (AOM).","more":"Explanation:\nFever, otalgia/irritability, otorrhea and a congested, bulging tympanic membrane strongly support acute otitis media (AOM). A bulging tympanic membrane is a key diagnostic feature; purulent otorrhea may occur after spontaneous perforation. Streptococcus pneumoniae is an important common bacterial cause, alongside non-typeable Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis. Otitis externa causes canal inflammation and pain with tragal/pinna movement; mastoiditis causes postauricular tenderness/swelling and displaced pinna.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe diagnosis depends on middle-ear effusion and acute inflammation; redness alone is not enough.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q5 — Acute otitis media after an upper respiratory infection","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 97 · answer PDF p. 98 · original question Q5","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q50","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"An absolute indication of tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy in children is: (June 201l)","o":["Recurrent tonsillitis.","Obstructive sleep apnea.","Recurrent otitis media.","Enlarged tonsillar LNs."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Adenotonsillectomy is a common first-line treatment for otherwise appropriate children with obstructive sleep apnea associated with adenotonsillar hypertrophy.","more":"Explanation:\nAdenotonsillectomy is a common first-line treatment for otherwise appropriate children with obstructive sleep apnea associated with adenotonsillar hypertrophy. Recurrent tonsillitis and recurrent otitis media have separate criteria and are not automatic absolute indications in every child. Enlarged lymph nodes alone do not indicate surgery.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Recurrent tonsillitis.** — Recurrent tonsillitis and otitis media require defined episode/complication criteria; lymph-node enlargement alone is not an indication.\n- **b. Obstructive sleep apnea.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Obstructive sleep apnea is the strongest indication among the listed options.\n- **c. Recurrent otitis media.** — Recurrent tonsillitis and otitis media require defined episode/complication criteria; lymph-node enlargement alone is not an indication.\n- **d. Enlarged tonsillar LNs.** — Recurrent tonsillitis and otitis media require defined episode/complication criteria; lymph-node enlargement alone is not an indication.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAdenotonsillectomy is a common first-line treatment for otherwise appropriate children with obstructive sleep apnea associated with adenotonsillar hypertrophy.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q50 — Obstructive sleep apnea is an indication for adenotonsillectomy","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 108 · answer PDF p. 108 · original question Q50","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q51","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Features","q":"Bronchial asthma characterized by all the following except:","o":["Hyperactive airway.","Clubbing.","Spasmodic cough.","Bilateral chest wheezes."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Asthma causes variable airflow obstruction, airway hyperresponsiveness and episodic cough/wheeze.","more":"Explanation:\nAsthma causes variable airflow obstruction, airway hyperresponsiveness and episodic cough/wheeze. Digital clubbing is not expected in uncomplicated asthma and should prompt evaluation for bronchiectasis, cystic fibrosis, chronic suppurative lung disease, interstitial disease or other systemic conditions.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Hyperactive airway.** — Airway hyperreactivity, spasmodic cough and bilateral wheeze are compatible with asthma.\n- **b. Clubbing.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Clubbing is the exception.\n- **c. Spasmodic cough.** — Airway hyperreactivity, spasmodic cough and bilateral wheeze are compatible with asthma.\n- **d. Bilateral chest wheezes.** — Airway hyperreactivity, spasmodic cough and bilateral wheeze are compatible with asthma.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAsthma causes variable airflow obstruction, airway hyperresponsiveness and episodic cough/wheeze.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q51 — Digital clubbing is not a feature of uncomplicated asthma","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 108 · answer PDF p. 108 · original question Q51","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q52","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"Clubbing of fingers is associated with the following conditions, except:","o":["Bronchial asthma.","Ulcerative colitis.","Cystic fibrosis.","Bronchiectasis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Digital clubbing is associated with chronic suppurative pulmonary disease such as cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis, and with non-pulmonary disorders such as inflammatory bowel disease.","more":"Explanation:\nDigital clubbing is associated with chronic suppurative pulmonary disease such as cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis, and with non-pulmonary disorders such as inflammatory bowel disease. It is not a feature of uncomplicated asthma; if present, look for another diagnosis or complication.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Bronchial asthma.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Bronchial asthma is the exception.\n- **b. Ulcerative colitis.** — Ulcerative colitis, cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis can be associated with clubbing.\n- **c. Cystic fibrosis.** — Ulcerative colitis, cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis can be associated with clubbing.\n- **d. Bronchiectasis.** — Ulcerative colitis, cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis can be associated with clubbing.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nDigital clubbing is associated with chronic suppurative pulmonary disease such as cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis, and with non-pulmonary disorders such as inflammatory bowel disease.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q52 — Clubbing occurs in CF and bronchiectasis, not asthma","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 108 · answer PDF p. 108 · original question Q52","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q53","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Investigation","q":"A previously healthy 4 years old boy presented with difficult breathing for one day. Three days prior, he had developed a runny nose, cough and fever (40°C). On examination, he is awake, alert, in moderate distress, with decreased air entry over the right lower lobe with fine crepitations. His lab tests revealed leucocytosis with raised","o":["SR. What is the appropriate next step in management:","Blood culture.","Chest x-ray.","IM antibiotics.","IV antibiotics."],"ol":["E","A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"A febrile child with focal reduced air entry and crackles may have pneumonia.","more":"Explanation:\nA febrile child with focal reduced air entry and crackles may have pneumonia. Chest radiography is useful when diagnosis is uncertain, disease is significant/hospitalized, complications are suspected or response is poor; it is not required for every uncomplicated outpatient case. The stem’s focal findings and need to clarify disease location support imaging before selecting a definitive intervention.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Blood culture.** — Blood culture may be considered in hospitalized severe disease but does not localize the pulmonary process; antibiotics are treatment, not the next diagnostic step; IM antibiotics are not the preferred approach for an ill child requiring assessment.\n- **b. Chest x-ray.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Chest radiograph is the most useful next investigation among the listed options in this scenario.\n- **c. IM antibiotics.** — Blood culture may be considered in hospitalized severe disease but does not localize the pulmonary process; antibiotics are treatment, not the next diagnostic step; IM antibiotics are not the preferred approach for an ill child requiring assessment.\n- **d. IV antibiotics.** — Blood culture may be considered in hospitalized severe disease but does not localize the pulmonary process; antibiotics are treatment, not the next diagnostic step; IM antibiotics are not the preferred approach for an ill child requiring assessment.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA febrile child with focal reduced air entry and crackles may have pneumonia.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q53 — Chest radiograph is the next investigation for suspected pneumonia","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 108 · answer PDF p. 108 · original question Q53","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q54","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Investigation","q":"A 5-year-old boy presents with a 1-week-history of fever, anorexia, productive cough, and right upper quadrant abdominal pain. On examination, he is febrile, short of breath, with mild subcostal and intercostal recessions. Auscultation reveals decreased breath sounds on the right side ofher chest. What is the most likely helpful investigation:","o":["Liver enzymes.","Chest x-ray.","Abdominal ultrasound.","Tuberculin test."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Lower-lobe pneumonia can present with abdominal pain because of diaphragmatic/pleural irritation.","more":"Explanation:\nLower-lobe pneumonia can present with abdominal pain because of diaphragmatic/pleural irritation. In a febrile child with cough, dyspnea and reduced unilateral air entry, a chest radiograph is the most helpful listed investigation. Liver enzymes, abdominal ultrasound and TST do not directly confirm the suspected respiratory pathology.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Liver enzymes.** — Liver enzymes and abdominal ultrasound investigate hepatobiliary/abdominal disease; TST is not a test to diagnose acute pneumonia.\n- **b. Chest x-ray.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Chest radiograph.\n- **c. Abdominal ultrasound.** — Liver enzymes and abdominal ultrasound investigate hepatobiliary/abdominal disease; TST is not a test to diagnose acute pneumonia.\n- **d. Tuberculin test.** — Liver enzymes and abdominal ultrasound investigate hepatobiliary/abdominal disease; TST is not a test to diagnose acute pneumonia.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nLower-lobe pneumonia can present with abdominal pain because of diaphragmatic/pleural irritation.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q54 — Chest radiograph is useful when pneumonia presents with abdominal pain","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 108 · answer PDF p. 108 · original question Q54","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q55","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"Regarding acute bronchiolitis, which of the following statements is wrong:","o":["Mainly caused by adenovirus.","Mainly occurs during first 2 years of life.","Clinically, the child shows respiratory distress and expiratory wheezing.","Case fatality rate is less than 1%."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"RSV is the most frequent cause of bronchiolitis in infants and young children.","more":"Explanation:\nRSV is the most frequent cause of bronchiolitis in infants and young children. Bronchiolitis mainly affects children under 2 years and presents with respiratory distress, cough, wheeze and crackles. Most otherwise healthy children recover; case-fatality is low in high-resource settings but risk is higher in premature infants and those with comorbidities.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Mainly caused by adenovirus.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. The false statement is “mainly caused by adenovirus”; RSV is the leading cause.\n- **b. Mainly occurs during first 2 years of life.** — Age and clinical features are consistent with bronchiolitis; case-fatality is generally low in healthy children, though risk varies by setting and comorbidity.\n- **c. Clinically, the child shows respiratory distress and expiratory wheezing.** — Age and clinical features are consistent with bronchiolitis; case-fatality is generally low in healthy children, though risk varies by setting and comorbidity.\n- **d. Case fatality rate is less than 1%.** — Age and clinical features are consistent with bronchiolitis; case-fatality is generally low in healthy children, though risk varies by setting and comorbidity.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nRSV is the most frequent cause of bronchiolitis in infants and young children.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q55 — RSV rather than adenovirus is the leading cause of bronchiolitis","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 108 · answer PDF p. 108 · original question Q55","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q56","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Regarding cystic fibrosis which of the following is wrong:","o":["A gene located on chromosome 7 is defective.","Most patients have pancreatic endocrine problem.","There is excessive concentrated NaCl in sweat.","Respiratory secretions are higher in viscosity."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Most classic CF patients with pancreatic-insufficient disease develop exocrine pancreatic dysfunction, causing malabsorption and steatorrhea.","more":"Explanation:\nMost classic CF patients with pancreatic-insufficient disease develop exocrine pancreatic dysfunction, causing malabsorption and steatorrhea. Pancreatic endocrine damage and CF-related diabetes may occur later in some patients but is not the predominant pancreatic manifestation in most children. CFTR lies on chromosome 7, sweat chloride is elevated, and airway mucus is viscous.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. A gene located on chromosome 7 is defective.** — CFTR is on chromosome 7; sweat chloride is increased; airway secretions are viscous.\n- **b. Most patients have pancreatic endocrine problem.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. The false statement is that most patients have a pancreatic endocrine problem; exocrine insufficiency is more typical.\n- **c. There is excessive concentrated NaCl in sweat.** — CFTR is on chromosome 7; sweat chloride is increased; airway secretions are viscous.\n- **d. Respiratory secretions are higher in viscosity.** — CFTR is on chromosome 7; sweat chloride is increased; airway secretions are viscous.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nMost classic CF patients with pancreatic-insufficient disease develop exocrine pancreatic dysfunction, causing malabsorption and steatorrhea.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q56 — Cystic fibrosis predominantly causes pancreatic exocrine insufficiency","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 109 · answer PDF p. 109 · original question Q56","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q57","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Features","q":"The most indicative signs of pneumonia in l-year-old child is:","o":["Fever and retractions.","Grunting and breathing difficulty.","Fever and rapid respiration.","Audible wheezing."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"In children, age-specific tachypnea is an important screening sign for pneumonia, especially when accompanied by fever and cough.","more":"Explanation:\nIn children, age-specific tachypnea is an important screening sign for pneumonia, especially when accompanied by fever and cough. Grunting, retractions and hypoxemia suggest more severe disease. Wheeze alone is less specific and can occur in viral bronchiolitis or asthma. No single sign establishes pneumonia without clinical context.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Fever and retractions.** — Retractions/grunting indicate respiratory distress but are less specific for pneumonia; wheeze occurs in several airway disorders.\n- **b. Grunting and breathing difficulty.** — Retractions/grunting indicate respiratory distress but are less specific for pneumonia; wheeze occurs in several airway disorders.\n- **c. Fever and rapid respiration.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Fever with rapid respiration is a key clinical clue to pneumonia.\n- **d. Audible wheezing.** — Retractions/grunting indicate respiratory distress but are less specific for pneumonia; wheeze occurs in several airway disorders.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nIn children, age-specific tachypnea is an important screening sign for pneumonia, especially when accompanied by fever and cough.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q57 — Tachypnea with fever is a sensitive clinical clue to childhood pneumonia","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 109 · answer PDF p. 109 · original question Q57","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q58","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Genetics / Pathophysiology","q":"Cystic fibrosis is characterized by all except:","o":["The concentration of sodium and chloride in sweat is abnormally high.","Recurrent chest infection is common.","Autosomal dominant.","Gene located on chromosome 7 is defective."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"CF is caused by pathogenic variants in both copies of the CFTR gene on chromosome 7 and is inherited in an autosomal-recessive pattern.","more":"Explanation:\nCF is caused by pathogenic variants in both copies of the CFTR gene on chromosome 7 and is inherited in an autosomal-recessive pattern. High sweat chloride, recurrent respiratory infection and pancreatic exocrine insufficiency are classic features.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. The concentration of sodium and chloride in sweat is abnormally high.** — High sweat sodium/chloride, recurrent infections and CFTR location on chromosome 7 are consistent with CF.\n- **b. Recurrent chest infection is common.** — High sweat sodium/chloride, recurrent infections and CFTR location on chromosome 7 are consistent with CF.\n- **c. Autosomal dominant.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Autosomal-dominant inheritance is the false statement.\n- **d. Gene located on chromosome 7 is defective.** — High sweat sodium/chloride, recurrent infections and CFTR location on chromosome 7 are consistent with CF.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nCF is caused by pathogenic variants in both copies of the CFTR gene on chromosome 7 and is inherited in an autosomal-recessive pattern.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q58 — Cystic fibrosis is autosomal recessive, not autosomal dominant","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 109 · answer PDF p. 109 · original question Q58","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q59","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"Which of the following is true about bronchiolitis:","o":["Never occurs after the age of 6 years.","Most cases are caused by RSV.","Antibiotics are recommended in infants below 3 months.","Recurrence is very common."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"RSV is the major cause of bronchiolitis in infancy.","more":"Explanation:\nRSV is the major cause of bronchiolitis in infancy. It can occur after 6 years but is much less typical at older ages. Antibiotics are not recommended solely because an infant is younger than 3 months; recurrence is possible but is not “very common” as a defining feature.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Never occurs after the age of 6 years.** — Bronchiolitis is not strictly impossible after 6 years; antibiotics require a bacterial indication; recurrence is not the defining feature.\n- **b. Most cases are caused by RSV.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Most bronchiolitis cases are caused by RSV.\n- **c. Antibiotics are recommended in infants below 3 months.** — Bronchiolitis is not strictly impossible after 6 years; antibiotics require a bacterial indication; recurrence is not the defining feature.\n- **d. Recurrence is very common.** — Bronchiolitis is not strictly impossible after 6 years; antibiotics require a bacterial indication; recurrence is not the defining feature.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nRSV is the major cause of bronchiolitis in infancy.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q59 — RSV is the leading bronchiolitis pathogen","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 109 · answer PDF p. 109 · original question Q59","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q6","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"The dose ofIM streptomycin (an anti-tuberculous drugs) is:","o":["5-8 mg/kg/day.","10-20 mg/kg/day.","20-40 mg/kg/day.","40-60 mg/kg/day."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Streptomycin is an injectable aminoglycoside and is now reserved for selected drug-resistant or special TB situations rather than routine first-line treatment.","more":"Explanation:\nStreptomycin is an injectable aminoglycoside and is now reserved for selected drug-resistant or special TB situations rather than routine first-line treatment. A single broad historical dose range cannot be applied safely without age, renal function, maximum dose, dosing interval, susceptibility profile and the current TB regimen. Contemporary pediatric drug-susceptible TB regimens generally use combinations built around isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol; regimen selection needs TB expertise.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. 5-8 mg/kg/day.** — The dose alternatives are broad, and the question omits clinical variables required for safe aminoglycoside dosing.\n- **b. 10-20 mg/kg/day.** — The dose alternatives are broad, and the question omits clinical variables required for safe aminoglycoside dosing.\n- **c. 20-40 mg/kg/day.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. The options do not establish a universally safe current streptomycin dose; the printed key is not sufficient for prescribing.\n- **d. 40-60 mg/kg/day.** — The dose alternatives are broad, and the question omits clinical variables required for safe aminoglycoside dosing.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nStreptomycin carries nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity risks and requires careful monitoring.\n\nReview note:\nDo not use this historical key for clinical dosing. Verify the original lecturer source and the applicable local TB guideline.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q6 — Historical streptomycin dose: range is unsafe to apply without a current TB protocol","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 98 · answer PDF p. 98 · original question Q6","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q60","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"In bronchial asthma which statement is wrong:","o":["Chronic inflammatory disease in the airways.","Self-limited disease (most patients recover before 6 years of age).","Viral respiratory infections are among important asthma triggers.","Has polygenetic (multifactorial) inheritance."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Asthma is a chronic heterogeneous inflammatory airway disorder with variable symptoms and variable expiratory airflow limitation.","more":"Explanation:\nAsthma is a chronic heterogeneous inflammatory airway disorder with variable symptoms and variable expiratory airflow limitation. Some children become asymptomatic for prolonged periods, but asthma is not accurately described as a self-limited disease in which most recover before age six. Viral respiratory infections are common triggers and genetic predisposition is multifactorial/polygenic.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Chronic inflammatory disease in the airways.** — Chronic airway inflammation, viral triggers and multifactorial inheritance are consistent with asthma.\n- **b. Self-limited disease (most patients recover before 6 years of age).** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. The self-limited-before-six statement is false.\n- **c. Viral respiratory infections are among important asthma triggers.** — Chronic airway inflammation, viral triggers and multifactorial inheritance are consistent with asthma.\n- **d. Has polygenetic (multifactorial) inheritance.** — Chronic airway inflammation, viral triggers and multifactorial inheritance are consistent with asthma.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAsthma is a chronic heterogeneous inflammatory airway disorder with variable symptoms and variable expiratory airflow limitation.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q60 — Asthma is chronic and is not usually outgrown by age six","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 109 · answer PDF p. 109 · original question Q60","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q61","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"Asthma triggers include all the following except:","o":["Viral respiratory infections.","Tobacco smoke.","House dust mite.","Steroids."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Common asthma triggers include viral respiratory infections, tobacco smoke, house-dust mite and other allergens/irritants.","more":"Explanation:\nCommon asthma triggers include viral respiratory infections, tobacco smoke, house-dust mite and other allergens/irritants. Systemic corticosteroids are used to treat significant exacerbations and are not a standard asthma trigger; inhaled corticosteroids are foundational controller therapy.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Viral respiratory infections.** — Viral infections, tobacco smoke and dust mite exposure can precipitate symptoms/exacerbations.\n- **b. Tobacco smoke.** — Viral infections, tobacco smoke and dust mite exposure can precipitate symptoms/exacerbations.\n- **c. House dust mite.** — Viral infections, tobacco smoke and dust mite exposure can precipitate symptoms/exacerbations.\n- **d. Steroids.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Steroids are the exception among the listed triggers.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nCommon asthma triggers include viral respiratory infections, tobacco smoke, house-dust mite and other allergens/irritants.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q61 — Systemic steroids are not an asthma trigger","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 109 · answer PDF p. 109 · original question Q61","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q62","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Prevention","q":"Regarding childhood bronchial asthma, which of the following statements is wrong:","o":["It is a chronic inflammatory disease of the airways.","All asthmatic children should be advised to avoid certain foods e.g. eggs, chocolate.","Viral respiratory infections are among the important asthma triggers.","Most patients need controller medications as well as bronchodilators."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Food allergy can trigger symptoms in a subset of patients with confirmed food allergy, but all children with asthma should not be advised to avoid eggs, chocolate or other foods without a specific documented allergy.","more":"Explanation:\nFood allergy can trigger symptoms in a subset of patients with confirmed food allergy, but all children with asthma should not be advised to avoid eggs, chocolate or other foods without a specific documented allergy. Viral infections are important triggers, and many children with persistent asthma require controller treatment in addition to reliever medication.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. It is a chronic inflammatory disease of the airways.** — Chronic inflammation, viral triggers and controller-plus-reliever strategies are standard concepts; food elimination is individualized only for confirmed allergy.\n- **b. All asthmatic children should be advised to avoid certain foods e.g. eggs, chocolate.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Universal avoidance of eggs/chocolate is not recommended in all asthmatic children.\n- **c. Viral respiratory infections are among the important asthma triggers.** — Chronic inflammation, viral triggers and controller-plus-reliever strategies are standard concepts; food elimination is individualized only for confirmed allergy.\n- **d. Most patients need controller medications as well as bronchodilators.** — Chronic inflammation, viral triggers and controller-plus-reliever strategies are standard concepts; food elimination is individualized only for confirmed allergy.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nFood allergy can trigger symptoms in a subset of patients with confirmed food allergy, but all children with asthma should not be advised to avoid eggs, chocolate or other foods without a specific documented allergy.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q62 — Routine food avoidance is not recommended for every child with asthma","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 109 · answer PDF p. 109 · original question Q62","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q63-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"A 6-year-old asthmatic child presented to ER with severe grade III respiratory distress, bilateral diminished air entry and generalized wheezing. Oxygen saturation at room air is 88%. Part I: The most appropriate 1st step is:","o":["Ask for plain chest x-ray.","Oxygen and nebulized salbutamol.","Inhaled corticosteroids.","IV steroids."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"A child with severe distress, bilateral reduced air entry, wheeze and SpO₂ 88% needs urgent emergency management.","more":"Explanation:\nA child with severe distress, bilateral reduced air entry, wheeze and SpO₂ 88% needs urgent emergency management. Give oxygen for hypoxemia and repeated inhaled/nebulized short-acting beta-2 agonist (salbutamol); add ipratropium and early systemic corticosteroids in a severe attack. A chest radiograph is not the first routine step unless a complication or alternative diagnosis is suspected. A “jet-black” hyperlucent chest radiograph is not an expected defining finding of ordinary asthma; hyperinflation may be seen.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA child with severe distress, bilateral reduced air entry, wheeze and SpO₂ 88% needs urgent emergency management.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q63 — Severe asthma exacerbation: oxygen and inhaled salbutamol are immediate measures","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 110 · answer PDF p. 110 · original question Q63","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q63-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"A 6-year-old asthmatic child presented to ER with severe grade III respiratory distress, bilateral diminished air entry and generalized wheezing. Oxygen saturation at room air is 88%. Part II: Which of the following is unlikely:","o":["Positive family history of atopy.","Eosinophilia in CBC.","High total serum IgE.","Jet black hyper translucency in chest x-ray."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"A child with severe distress, bilateral reduced air entry, wheeze and SpO₂ 88% needs urgent emergency management.","more":"Explanation:\nA child with severe distress, bilateral reduced air entry, wheeze and SpO₂ 88% needs urgent emergency management. Give oxygen for hypoxemia and repeated inhaled/nebulized short-acting beta-2 agonist (salbutamol); add ipratropium and early systemic corticosteroids in a severe attack. A chest radiograph is not the first routine step unless a complication or alternative diagnosis is suspected. A “jet-black” hyperlucent chest radiograph is not an expected defining finding of ordinary asthma; hyperinflation may be seen.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA child with severe distress, bilateral reduced air entry, wheeze and SpO₂ 88% needs urgent emergency management.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q63 — Severe asthma exacerbation: oxygen and inhaled salbutamol are immediate measures","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 110 · answer PDF p. 110 · original question Q63","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q64","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"Long-term controller medications for asthma include all except:","o":["Inhaled corticosteroids as Fluticasone.","Leukotriene modifiers as Montelukast.","Mast cell stabilizers as sodium cromoglycate.","Short acting beta adrenergic as salbutamol."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Inhaled corticosteroids are the core anti-inflammatory controller treatment for many children.","more":"Explanation:\nInhaled corticosteroids are the core anti-inflammatory controller treatment for many children. Leukotriene receptor antagonists are alternative/add-on controllers in selected cases. Sodium cromoglycate is an older mast-cell stabilizer controller option with limited contemporary use. Salbutamol is a short-acting beta-2 agonist reliever and does not provide anti-inflammatory long-term control.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Inhaled corticosteroids as Fluticasone.** — ICS, montelukast and sodium cromoglycate are controller/preventer categories; salbutamol relieves acute bronchoconstriction.\n- **b. Leukotriene modifiers as Montelukast.** — ICS, montelukast and sodium cromoglycate are controller/preventer categories; salbutamol relieves acute bronchoconstriction.\n- **c. Mast cell stabilizers as sodium cromoglycate.** — ICS, montelukast and sodium cromoglycate are controller/preventer categories; salbutamol relieves acute bronchoconstriction.\n- **d. Short acting beta adrenergic as salbutamol.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Short-acting beta-agonist salbutamol is the exception.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nInhaled corticosteroids are the core anti-inflammatory controller treatment for many children.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q64 — Salbutamol is a reliever, not a long-term controller","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 110 · answer PDF p. 110 · original question Q64","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q65","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Regarding childhood bronchial asthma, which of the following statements is wrong:","o":["The only mechanism of airway obstruction is bronchoconstriction (bronchospasm).","During acute attack, chest is hyperinflated.","Aspirin and NSAIDs are triggers of asthma.","Prostaglandin Ei."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Asthma airflow limitation is not caused only by bronchoconstriction.","more":"Explanation:\nAsthma airflow limitation is not caused only by bronchoconstriction. Airway inflammation, mucosal edema, mucus hypersecretion and airway remodeling also contribute. During an attack, air trapping can cause hyperinflation, and aspirin/NSAIDs can trigger bronchospasm in susceptible patients. However, option d appears as the incomplete OCR fragment “Prostaglandin Ei.”, so the whole “wrong statement” item cannot be reliably validated from this text alone.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. The only mechanism of airway obstruction is bronchoconstriction (bronchospasm).** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Option a is clearly false because bronchoconstriction is not the only mechanism; the incomplete option d prevents certainty that it is the unique false option.\n- **b. During acute attack, chest is hyperinflated.** — The unlabelled/incomplete prostaglandin fragment cannot be analyzed confidently; all complete options are interpreted as written.\n- **c. Aspirin and NSAIDs are triggers of asthma.** — The unlabelled/incomplete prostaglandin fragment cannot be analyzed confidently; all complete options are interpreted as written.\n- **d. Prostaglandin Ei.** — The unlabelled/incomplete prostaglandin fragment cannot be analyzed confidently; all complete options are interpreted as written.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAsthma airflow limitation is not caused only by bronchoconstriction.\n\nReview note:\nCheck the original PDF for the complete wording of option d before finalizing the one-best-answer key.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q65 — Asthma obstruction mechanisms: incomplete final option prevents full verification","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 110 · answer PDF p. 110 · original question Q65","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q66","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Investigation","q":"Blood picture of a child with atopic bronchial asthma commonly shows:","o":["Neutrophils.","Eosinophils.","Monocytosis.","Lymphocytosis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Allergic/type 2 airway inflammation may be associated with peripheral eosinophilia and elevated IgE, although neither is required for diagnosis and normal values do not exclude asthma.","more":"Explanation:\nAllergic/type 2 airway inflammation may be associated with peripheral eosinophilia and elevated IgE, although neither is required for diagnosis and normal values do not exclude asthma. Eosinophils are the best answer among the listed blood-cell patterns for atopic asthma.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Neutrophils.** — Neutrophilia, monocytosis and lymphocytosis are not the characteristic type 2 allergic-asthma association.\n- **b. Eosinophils.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Eosinophils.\n- **c. Monocytosis.** — Neutrophilia, monocytosis and lymphocytosis are not the characteristic type 2 allergic-asthma association.\n- **d. Lymphocytosis.** — Neutrophilia, monocytosis and lymphocytosis are not the characteristic type 2 allergic-asthma association.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAllergic/type 2 airway inflammation may be associated with peripheral eosinophilia and elevated IgE, although neither is required for diagnosis and normal values do not exclude asthma.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q66 — Atopic asthma is commonly associated with eosinophilia","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 110 · answer PDF p. 110 · original question Q66","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q67","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Genetics","q":"Regarding cystic fibrosis, which of the following is wrong:","o":["Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency occurs in most patients.","Results from a defect in a gene located on chromosome number 9.","Recurrent chest infections are common.","There is increase in the sodium and chloride levels in the sweat."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"The CFTR gene is located on chromosome 7 (7q31.2).","more":"Explanation:\nThe CFTR gene is located on chromosome 7 (7q31.2). Pathogenic variants cause cystic fibrosis, an autosomal-recessive disorder associated with elevated sweat chloride, recurrent respiratory infection and pancreatic exocrine insufficiency.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency occurs in most patients.** — Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency, recurrent chest infections and elevated sweat sodium/chloride are characteristic of CF.\n- **b. Results from a defect in a gene located on chromosome number 9.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. Chromosome 9 is incorrect; CFTR is on chromosome 7.\n- **c. Recurrent chest infections are common.** — Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency, recurrent chest infections and elevated sweat sodium/chloride are characteristic of CF.\n- **d. There is increase in the sodium and chloride levels in the sweat.** — Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency, recurrent chest infections and elevated sweat sodium/chloride are characteristic of CF.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe CFTR gene is located on chromosome 7 (7q31.2).","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q67 — CFTR gene is located on chromosome 7","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 110 · answer PDF p. 110 · original question Q67","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q68","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Features","q":"Regarding Pott's disease, which is wrong:","o":["Lower thoracic spine is more commonly affected than cervical spine.","May result in angular kyphosis.","Affects body of one or more vertebrae.","Tuberculin test is always negative."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Pott disease is spinal tuberculosis, commonly affecting thoracic or thoracolumbar vertebral bodies.","more":"Explanation:\nPott disease is spinal tuberculosis, commonly affecting thoracic or thoracolumbar vertebral bodies. It can cause vertebral destruction, collapse and angular kyphosis. A tuberculin skin test may be positive, but it can also be negative in severe/disseminated disease or immunosuppression; it is not “always negative.”\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Lower thoracic spine is more commonly affected than cervical spine.** — Thoracic involvement is common; vertebral-body disease and angular kyphosis are recognized features.\n- **b. May result in angular kyphosis.** — Thoracic involvement is common; vertebral-body disease and angular kyphosis are recognized features.\n- **c. Affects body of one or more vertebrae.** — Thoracic involvement is common; vertebral-body disease and angular kyphosis are recognized features.\n- **d. Tuberculin test is always negative.** — This is the choice identified by the printed source key. The statement that TST is always negative is false.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nPott disease is spinal tuberculosis, commonly affecting thoracic or thoracolumbar vertebral bodies.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q68 — Pott disease: spinal tuberculosis can have a positive tuberculin test","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 110 · answer PDF p. 111 · original question Q68","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q7-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 4-year-old girl presented to paediatrician due to chronic ill health (the exact terms of mother). On examination, child's body weight was 12 kg (below 3rd percentile) and height was 89 cm (below 3rd percentile). The mother said that her girl used to have very recurrent chest infections and almost never cough-free. She describes her cough to be loose and productive. The girl also had frequent afebrile diarrhea. At her neonatal period, she had prolonged neonatal jaundice. Part I: The most likely diagnosis is:","o":["Chronic persistent asthma.","Lactose intolerance.","Cystic fibrosis."],"ol":["A","B","C"],"k":2,"ex":"Cystic fibrosis is an autosomal-recessive CFTR disorder causing dehydrated, thick secretions.","more":"Explanation:\nCystic fibrosis is an autosomal-recessive CFTR disorder causing dehydrated, thick secretions. Chronic suppurative airway disease, poor growth, pancreatic exocrine insufficiency and fat malabsorption are characteristic. Pancreatic insufficiency produces bulky, greasy, offensive stools (steatorrhea). Prolonged neonatal jaundice can also occur, for example with neonatal cholestasis.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nCystic fibrosis is an autosomal-recessive CFTR disorder causing dehydrated, thick secretions.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q7 — Cystic fibrosis: recurrent chest infection, failure to thrive and steatorrhea","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 98 · answer PDF p. 98 · original question Q7","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q7-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 4-year-old girl presented to paediatrician due to chronic ill health (the exact terms of mother). On examination, child's body weight was 12 kg (below 3rd percentile) and height was 89 cm (below 3rd percentile). The mother said that her girl used to have very recurrent chest infections and almost never cough-free. She describes her cough to be loose and productive. The girl also had frequent afebrile diarrhea. At her neonatal period, she had prolonged neonatal jaundice. Part II: The type of diarrhea that is usually seen in these patients is:","o":["Bloody diarrhea.","Watery diarrhea.","Steatorrhea.","Starvation diarrhea."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Cystic fibrosis is an autosomal-recessive CFTR disorder causing dehydrated, thick secretions.","more":"Explanation:\nCystic fibrosis is an autosomal-recessive CFTR disorder causing dehydrated, thick secretions. Chronic suppurative airway disease, poor growth, pancreatic exocrine insufficiency and fat malabsorption are characteristic. Pancreatic insufficiency produces bulky, greasy, offensive stools (steatorrhea). Prolonged neonatal jaundice can also occur, for example with neonatal cholestasis.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nCystic fibrosis is an autosomal-recessive CFTR disorder causing dehydrated, thick secretions.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q7 — Cystic fibrosis: recurrent chest infection, failure to thrive and steatorrhea","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 98 · answer PDF p. 98 · original question Q7","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q8-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"An 8-year-old girl was diagnosed to have bacterial pneumonia since 4 days. She was hospitalized and received IV ampicillin. Today, while in hospital, her temperature went up to 39.8°C, her respiratory distress increased significantly, the right lung is stony dull on percussion and trachea is significantly shifted to the left side. Oxygen saturation in room air is 90%. Part I: What is the most probable cause of this deterioration:","o":["Right massive empyema.","Right tension pneumothorax.","Right lung abscess.","Type I respiratory failure."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Deterioration during pneumonia with high fever, increasing respiratory distress, a stony-dull hemithorax and contralateral tracheal shift suggests a large pleural collection, most likely empyema/parapneumonic effusion.","more":"Explanation:\nDeterioration during pneumonia with high fever, increasing respiratory distress, a stony-dull hemithorax and contralateral tracheal shift suggests a large pleural collection, most likely empyema/parapneumonic effusion. Oxygen and urgent assessment are priorities. Imaging (often chest radiograph and/or ultrasound) characterizes the collection; moderate/large effusions with distress or purulent fluid generally require pleural drainage. CBC is useful but is less urgent than stabilization and evaluation of the pleural emergency.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nFor pediatric empyema requiring drainage, the 2026 IDSA/PIDS guideline favors chest tube drainage with intrapleural fibrinolytic therapy over immediate surgical debridement in most cases.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q8 — Pneumonia complicated by a large right empyema","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 98 · answer PDF p. 98 · original question Q8","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q8-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"An 8-year-old girl was diagnosed to have bacterial pneumonia since 4 days. She was hospitalized and received IV ampicillin. Today, while in hospital, her temperature went up to 39.8°C, her respiratory distress increased significantly, the right lung is stony dull on percussion and trachea is significantly shifted to the left side. Oxygen saturation in room air is 90%. Part II: The least urgent intervention is:","o":["Oxygen therapy.","Intercostal tube for drainage.","Chest x-ray.","CBC."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Deterioration during pneumonia with high fever, increasing respiratory distress, a stony-dull hemithorax and contralateral tracheal shift suggests a large pleural collection, most likely empyema/parapneumonic effusion.","more":"Explanation:\nDeterioration during pneumonia with high fever, increasing respiratory distress, a stony-dull hemithorax and contralateral tracheal shift suggests a large pleural collection, most likely empyema/parapneumonic effusion. Oxygen and urgent assessment are priorities. Imaging (often chest radiograph and/or ultrasound) characterizes the collection; moderate/large effusions with distress or purulent fluid generally require pleural drainage. CBC is useful but is less urgent than stabilization and evaluation of the pleural emergency.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nFor pediatric empyema requiring drainage, the 2026 IDSA/PIDS guideline favors chest tube drainage with intrapleural fibrinolytic therapy over immediate surgical debridement in most cases.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q8 — Pneumonia complicated by a large right empyema","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 98 · answer PDF p. 98 · original question Q8","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q9-I","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 3-year-old boy was brought to endocrinology clinic because of failure to gain weight. His weight and height were below the 3rd percentile for age and sex. History was positive for abnormally recurrent chest infections, for which he was hospitalized 3 times in the last 4 months. The mother also states that her child used to have frequent large pale very offensive greasy stools. A similar condition was seen in his late brother who died at the age of 6 years. Part I: The most likely diagnosis is:","o":["Celiac disease.","Cow milk allergy.","Cystic fibrosis.","Lactose intolerance."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"The combination of poor growth, recurrent respiratory infections, steatorrhea and an affected sibling suggests autosomal-recessive cystic fibrosis.","more":"Explanation:\nThe combination of poor growth, recurrent respiratory infections, steatorrhea and an affected sibling suggests autosomal-recessive cystic fibrosis. CFTR dysfunction produces thick airway mucus and pancreatic duct obstruction, causing exocrine pancreatic insufficiency and malabsorption. Pancreatic enzyme replacement, fat-soluble vitamin assessment and nutritional support are important parts of care.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA quantitative sweat chloride test is the diagnostic standard; values ≥60 mmol/L support CF in the appropriate clinical context.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q9 — Cystic fibrosis with pancreatic exocrine insufficiency","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 99 · answer PDF p. 99 · original question Q9","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RESPIRATION-Q9-II","spec":"ao-respiration","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 3-year-old boy was brought to endocrinology clinic because of failure to gain weight. His weight and height were below the 3rd percentile for age and sex. History was positive for abnormally recurrent chest infections, for which he was hospitalized 3 times in the last 4 months. The mother also states that her child used to have frequent large pale very offensive greasy stools. A similar condition was seen in his late brother who died at the age of 6 years. Part II: The stool abnormalities are caused by:","o":["Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency.","IgE-mediated villous atrophy.","Deficiency of lactase enzyme.","Infantile cholestasis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The combination of poor growth, recurrent respiratory infections, steatorrhea and an affected sibling suggests autosomal-recessive cystic fibrosis.","more":"Explanation:\nThe combination of poor growth, recurrent respiratory infections, steatorrhea and an affected sibling suggests autosomal-recessive cystic fibrosis. CFTR dysfunction produces thick airway mucus and pancreatic duct obstruction, causing exocrine pancreatic insufficiency and malabsorption. Pancreatic enzyme replacement, fat-soluble vitamin assessment and nutritional support are important parts of care.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA quantitative sweat chloride test is the diagnostic standard; values ≥60 mmol/L support CF in the appropriate clinical context.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q9 — Cystic fibrosis with pancreatic exocrine insufficiency","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 99 · answer PDF p. 99 · original question Q9","aoSystem":"Respiration","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RHEUMATOLOGY-Q1-I","spec":"ao-rheumatology","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case / Diagnosis / Management","q":"A10 years old girl presented with fever of 38.3 that was intermittent in the last 2 months. During the same period, the girl used to suffer from painful swelling of elbow, knees and small joints of the hands. accompanied by restricted movement of the affected joints, joint affection used to be symmetric. the girl used to complain of pain in the back of the neck. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["juvenile SLE","systemic onset juvenile rheumatoid arthritis","poly articular JRA","pauciarticular JRA"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"The child has chronic inflammatory arthritis involving multiple joints, apparently symmetrically, with fever and cervical discomfort.","more":"Explanation:\nThe child has chronic inflammatory arthritis involving multiple joints, apparently symmetrically, with fever and cervical discomfort. Polyarticular JIA is characterized by involvement of five or more joints during the first six months and can affect both large and small joints. Systemic JIA is characterized by arthritis with a characteristic systemic inflammatory syndrome, especially fever (typically quotidian/spiking) and additional systemic manifestations such as evanescent rash, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly or serositis. The question describes intermittent fever over two months but does not report rash, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly or serositis, and it does not clearly state the number of involved joints. Thus the distinction between systemic-onset and polyarticular JIA cannot be settled from the stem alone.\n\nFor treatment, NSAIDs can provide symptomatic relief but are not adequate monotherapy for persistent active polyarthritis. Methotrexate is commonly used as the initial conventional DMARD for non-systemic polyarticular JIA. Intra-articular glucocorticoids may be used as adjuncts for selected joints, while systemic glucocorticoids may be used as short-term bridging in selected severe cases or to control systemic inflammation when necessary. For systemic JIA without macrophage activation syndrome, contemporary guidance favours early IL-1 or IL-6 inhibitor strategies when available and recommends against routine oral glucocorticoid monotherapy. The source answer may reflect an older teaching approach or an unstated systemic disease context; it should not be generalized.\n\nOptions analysis:\n**Part I — Diagnosis**\n\na. Juvenile SLE can cause fever and inflammatory polyarthritis, but the stem provides no supporting lupus features (e.g., rash, photosensitivity, oral ulcers, cytopenias, renal findings or relevant autoantibodies). It cannot be excluded entirely from the limited information, but it is not the best supported choice.\nb. Source answer: systemic-onset JRA/JIA. This is possible because fever accompanies arthritis, but the stem lacks the characteristic systemic features and fever pattern needed to establish this subtype confidently.\nc. Polyarticular JRA/JIA is plausible because several large and small joints are involved symmetrically. The number of joints and exact six-month classification window are not specified, so it cannot be confirmed.\nd. Pauciarticular (oligoarticular) JIA involves four or fewer joints in the first six months; the described involvement of elbows, knees and small joints of the hands suggests more than four joints, making this less likely if the description is literal.\n\n**Part II — Early treatment**\na. NSAIDs are useful for symptomatic relief and may be an adjunct, but are not sufficient as sole therapy for persistent active polyarthritis.\nb. Methotrexate is a conventional DMARD commonly used early for polyarticular JIA and persistent joint inflammation; it is a strong candidate if the intended diagnosis is non-systemic polyarticular disease.\nc. Source answer: systemic corticosteroids. They can be used as short-term bridging or for severe systemic disease, but routine systemic steroid monotherapy is not the preferred contemporary long-term strategy for joint-dominant polyarticular JIA; in systemic JIA without MAS, current guidance conditionally recommends against oral glucocorticoids as initial monotherapy.\nd. Intra-articular corticosteroids can be useful for selected active joints as adjunct therapy, but they are not the sole systemic treatment for multiple-joint disease.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe key distinction is systemic inflammation versus joint-count-defined polyarthritis. For polyarticular JIA, methotrexate is a common early DMARD; systemic steroids are generally limited and context-dependent.\n\nReview note:\nRetain both source answers unchanged. A complete clinical history (fever pattern, rash, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, serositis), exact joint count, inflammatory markers and relevant serology are needed to classify the subtype. The second answer depends on whether the intended disease is systemic JIA or non-systemic polyarticular JIA and reflects treatment guidance that has evolved. The classification remains NEEDS REVIEW pending the user's decision.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q1 — Fever, symmetric polyarthritis and the choice of early therapy in juvenile arthritis","aoSource":"PDF p. 153 · printed p. 152 (footer-derived) · answer PDF p. 153 · original Q1","aoSystem":"Rheumatology","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-RHEUMATOLOGY-Q1-II","spec":"ao-rheumatology","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case / Diagnosis / Management","q":"A10 years old girl presented with fever of 38.3 that was intermittent in the last 2 months. During the same period, the girl used to suffer from painful swelling of elbow, knees and small joints of the hands. accompanied by restricted movement of the affected joints, joint affection used to be symmetric. the girl used to complain of pain in the back of the neck. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["juvenile SLE","systemic onset juvenile rheumatoid arthritis","poly articular JRA","pauciarticular JRA"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"The child has chronic inflammatory arthritis involving multiple joints, apparently symmetrically, with fever and cervical discomfort.","more":"Explanation:\nThe child has chronic inflammatory arthritis involving multiple joints, apparently symmetrically, with fever and cervical discomfort. Polyarticular JIA is characterized by involvement of five or more joints during the first six months and can affect both large and small joints. Systemic JIA is characterized by arthritis with a characteristic systemic inflammatory syndrome, especially fever (typically quotidian/spiking) and additional systemic manifestations such as evanescent rash, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly or serositis. The question describes intermittent fever over two months but does not report rash, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly or serositis, and it does not clearly state the number of involved joints. Thus the distinction between systemic-onset and polyarticular JIA cannot be settled from the stem alone.\n\nFor treatment, NSAIDs can provide symptomatic relief but are not adequate monotherapy for persistent active polyarthritis. Methotrexate is commonly used as the initial conventional DMARD for non-systemic polyarticular JIA. Intra-articular glucocorticoids may be used as adjuncts for selected joints, while systemic glucocorticoids may be used as short-term bridging in selected severe cases or to control systemic inflammation when necessary. For systemic JIA without macrophage activation syndrome, contemporary guidance favours early IL-1 or IL-6 inhibitor strategies when available and recommends against routine oral glucocorticoid monotherapy. The source answer may reflect an older teaching approach or an unstated systemic disease context; it should not be generalized.\n\nOptions analysis:\n**Part I — Diagnosis**\n\na. Juvenile SLE can cause fever and inflammatory polyarthritis, but the stem provides no supporting lupus features (e.g., rash, photosensitivity, oral ulcers, cytopenias, renal findings or relevant autoantibodies). It cannot be excluded entirely from the limited information, but it is not the best supported choice.\nb. Source answer: systemic-onset JRA/JIA. This is possible because fever accompanies arthritis, but the stem lacks the characteristic systemic features and fever pattern needed to establish this subtype confidently.\nc. Polyarticular JRA/JIA is plausible because several large and small joints are involved symmetrically. The number of joints and exact six-month classification window are not specified, so it cannot be confirmed.\nd. Pauciarticular (oligoarticular) JIA involves four or fewer joints in the first six months; the described involvement of elbows, knees and small joints of the hands suggests more than four joints, making this less likely if the description is literal.\n\n**Part II — Early treatment**\na. NSAIDs are useful for symptomatic relief and may be an adjunct, but are not sufficient as sole therapy for persistent active polyarthritis.\nb. Methotrexate is a conventional DMARD commonly used early for polyarticular JIA and persistent joint inflammation; it is a strong candidate if the intended diagnosis is non-systemic polyarticular disease.\nc. Source answer: systemic corticosteroids. They can be used as short-term bridging or for severe systemic disease, but routine systemic steroid monotherapy is not the preferred contemporary long-term strategy for joint-dominant polyarticular JIA; in systemic JIA without MAS, current guidance conditionally recommends against oral glucocorticoids as initial monotherapy.\nd. Intra-articular corticosteroids can be useful for selected active joints as adjunct therapy, but they are not the sole systemic treatment for multiple-joint disease.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe key distinction is systemic inflammation versus joint-count-defined polyarthritis. For polyarticular JIA, methotrexate is a common early DMARD; systemic steroids are generally limited and context-dependent.\n\nReview note:\nRetain both source answers unchanged. A complete clinical history (fever pattern, rash, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, serositis), exact joint count, inflammatory markers and relevant serology are needed to classify the subtype. The second answer depends on whether the intended disease is systemic JIA or non-systemic polyarticular JIA and reflects treatment guidance that has evolved. The classification remains NEEDS REVIEW pending the user's decision.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q1 — Fever, symmetric polyarthritis and the choice of early therapy in juvenile arthritis","aoSource":"PDF p. 153 · printed p. 152 (footer-derived) · answer PDF p. 153 · original Q1","aoSystem":"Rheumatology","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-RHEUMATOLOGY-Q2","spec":"ao-rheumatology","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Features / Serology","q":"In juvenile rheumatoid arthritis, all the following are true except:","o":["Chronic anterior uveitis maybe a complication.","Early use of methotrexate reduces joint damage.","Rheumatoid factor is always positive.","May affect big and small joints."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Juvenile idiopathic arthritis is a heterogeneous group of chronic inflammatory arthritides beginning before age 16.","more":"Explanation:\nJuvenile idiopathic arthritis is a heterogeneous group of chronic inflammatory arthritides beginning before age 16. JIA subtypes differ in joint pattern, serology and extra-articular manifestations. Rheumatoid factor positivity is not required for diagnosis and is seen in only a minority of patients, particularly the RF-positive polyarticular category. Chronic anterior uveitis is an important extra-articular complication, particularly in ANA-positive oligoarticular and some RF-negative polyarticular disease. Methotrexate is a conventional disease-modifying antirheumatic drug used to control persistent synovitis and reduce ongoing inflammation and risk of joint damage. JIA may involve large joints, small joints or both depending on subtype.\n\nOptions analysis:\na. True: chronic anterior uveitis is a recognized complication of JIA; risk is especially associated with younger onset, ANA positivity and oligoarticular disease, though it can occur in other subtypes.\nb. True: early disease-modifying treatment, commonly methotrexate for persistent polyarthritis, helps control inflammation and limit cumulative joint damage.\nc. Correct “except”: rheumatoid factor is not always positive; most children with JIA are RF-negative.\nd. True: JIA can affect large and small joints; the pattern depends on subtype.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA negative rheumatoid factor does not exclude JIA. ANA is relevant to uveitis risk, while RF positivity defines only a minority subtype.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q2 — Rheumatoid factor is not always positive in juvenile idiopathic arthritis","aoSource":"PDF p. 153 · printed p. 152 (footer-derived) · answer PDF p. 153 · original Q2","aoSystem":"Rheumatology","aoPart":""}
